1
00:00:05,720 --> 00:00:09,280
So today, we're going to talk about evaluation.

2
00:00:09,280 --> 00:00:13,400
So far in this class we've done everything we

3
00:00:13,400 --> 00:00:15,680
need to train a language model.

4
00:00:15,680 --> 00:00:19,920
We defined the architecture, the optimizer, the main training

5
00:00:19,920 --> 00:00:20,540
loop.

6
00:00:20,540 --> 00:00:25,160
We showed how to make the training fast with kernels,

7
00:00:25,160 --> 00:00:26,840
parallelism.

8
00:00:26,840 --> 00:00:29,240
And then we saw scaling laws.

9
00:00:29,240 --> 00:00:33,560
We saw a little bit of how to make inference fast as well.

10
00:00:33,560 --> 00:00:36,840
So the only missing piece now is talking about the data

11
00:00:36,840 --> 00:00:38,880
that you train on.

12
00:00:38,880 --> 00:00:43,360
So we're going to spend next week talking about that.

13
00:00:43,360 --> 00:00:46,640
And data is going to shape the model behavior.

14
00:00:46,640 --> 00:00:49,520
If you train on code, then presumably

15
00:00:49,520 --> 00:00:51,380
your model is going to be good at code.

16
00:00:51,380 --> 00:00:53,240
If you train only on DNA sequences,

17
00:00:53,240 --> 00:00:56,320
it probably can't speak English.

18
00:00:56,320 --> 00:00:58,920
But before we even get to data, we

19
00:00:58,920 --> 00:01:02,170
need to talk about what behavior we want from a model,

20
00:01:02,170 --> 00:01:05,489
and that's going to be the topic of evaluation.

21
00:01:05,489 --> 00:01:08,290
So evaluation asks a very simple question--

22
00:01:08,290 --> 00:01:13,250
given the model that we've trained, how good is it?

23
00:01:13,250 --> 00:01:14,890
So what is good?

24
00:01:14,890 --> 00:01:19,770
So evaluation might seem like a fairly mechanical process.

25
00:01:19,770 --> 00:01:21,290
You define some prompts, you send

26
00:01:21,290 --> 00:01:23,870
these prompts to your model, you get back some responses,

27
00:01:23,870 --> 00:01:25,470
and then you compute the accuracy.

28
00:01:25,470 --> 00:01:29,010
So why do we have a whole lecture on evaluation?

29
00:01:29,010 --> 00:01:32,110
But actually, evaluation is a very deep topic,

30
00:01:32,110 --> 00:01:34,690
and it's also very important because evaluation is what

31
00:01:34,690 --> 00:01:36,650
shapes the development of AI.

32
00:01:36,650 --> 00:01:41,050
Evaluation sets North Stars and model developers--

33
00:01:41,050 --> 00:01:44,690
open, closed-- everyone looks at evaluation

34
00:01:44,690 --> 00:01:49,290
as a measure of progress.

35
00:01:49,290 --> 00:01:52,210
So by thinking carefully about your evaluation,

36
00:01:52,210 --> 00:01:54,610
you implicitly shape the development

37
00:01:54,610 --> 00:01:57,790
of what your model is going to be able to do.

38
00:01:57,790 --> 00:02:01,350
So what's hard about evaluation is the following.

39
00:02:01,350 --> 00:02:05,190
So often, you start it with an abstract construct of what

40
00:02:05,190 --> 00:02:07,150
do I want my model to do?

41
00:02:07,150 --> 00:02:11,630
Maybe I want my model to be good at conversation

42
00:02:11,630 --> 00:02:13,750
or good at reasoning.

43
00:02:13,750 --> 00:02:14,250
OK.

44
00:02:14,250 --> 00:02:16,230
So those are abstract concepts.

45
00:02:16,230 --> 00:02:18,470
And then evaluation is really the process

46
00:02:18,470 --> 00:02:23,030
of turning that into a concrete metric powered by, presumably,

47
00:02:23,030 --> 00:02:26,670
some concrete prompts or environments.

48
00:02:26,670 --> 00:02:29,930
So this is going to be the core challenge,

49
00:02:29,930 --> 00:02:32,270
and we'll see many examples of how

50
00:02:32,270 --> 00:02:38,070
this process of abstract construct, concrete metric shows up.

51
00:02:38,070 --> 00:02:42,070
So let's go back to a question of what makes a model good.

52
00:02:42,070 --> 00:02:44,430
Just kind of superficially.

53
00:02:44,430 --> 00:02:47,290
Maybe a model is good if it does well on benchmarks.

54
00:02:47,290 --> 00:02:49,890
So there's this website, Artificial Analysis,

55
00:02:49,890 --> 00:02:54,670
which has become a bit of a standard for thinking about

56
00:02:54,670 --> 00:02:57,200
quote unquote, "intelligence of models."

57
00:02:57,200 --> 00:02:59,840
So if you go to Artificial Analysis AI,

58
00:02:59,840 --> 00:03:03,920
you can see a ranking of different models from GPT-5.5,

59
00:03:03,920 --> 00:03:07,240
all the way down-- well, there's many more models after this

60
00:03:07,240 --> 00:03:09,640
point.

61
00:03:09,640 --> 00:03:13,110
And so this is a measure.

62
00:03:13,110 --> 00:03:16,000
It includes a bunch of different data sets, some of which

63
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:18,360
we'll talk about.

64
00:03:18,360 --> 00:03:20,960
Or maybe a model is good if it does well on benchmark

65
00:03:20,960 --> 00:03:26,160
and is also cheap to run, because cost should matter.

66
00:03:26,160 --> 00:03:30,440
And this plot shows both the intelligence index

67
00:03:30,440 --> 00:03:33,520
from before, but against how much money

68
00:03:33,520 --> 00:03:38,080
it costs to run a particular model based on inference cost.

69
00:03:38,080 --> 00:03:41,560
And you can see, of course, as you might expect,

70
00:03:41,560 --> 00:03:43,820
that the two are correlated.

71
00:03:43,820 --> 00:03:47,860
The more you pay to run, the higher the intelligence index,

72
00:03:47,860 --> 00:03:52,800
but it's not exactly aligned.

73
00:03:52,800 --> 00:03:57,770
But maybe a model is good if people just prefer it.

74
00:03:57,770 --> 00:04:00,150
Later we'll also go a little bit more into depth.

75
00:04:00,150 --> 00:04:04,010
So this is the take that Arena AI, formerly known

76
00:04:04,010 --> 00:04:06,290
as Chatbot Arena, takes.

77
00:04:06,290 --> 00:04:08,570
And you can form a ranking based on models,

78
00:04:08,570 --> 00:04:11,330
based on whether people like it or not.

79
00:04:11,330 --> 00:04:14,690
Or maybe a model is good if people simply

80
00:04:14,690 --> 00:04:17,870
choose to use it, and therefore, pay for it.

81
00:04:17,870 --> 00:04:22,010
So OpenRouter is this service that

82
00:04:22,010 --> 00:04:25,010
allows one to use multiple different models

83
00:04:25,010 --> 00:04:28,952
through a central endpoint.

84
00:04:28,952 --> 00:04:30,410
And as a result, they collect a lot

85
00:04:30,410 --> 00:04:34,410
of statistics of what models people are using.

86
00:04:34,410 --> 00:04:37,810
And they publish statistics, which

87
00:04:37,810 --> 00:04:39,330
is really interesting to look at,

88
00:04:39,330 --> 00:04:43,210
the top models that are used through OpenRouter.

89
00:04:43,210 --> 00:04:45,650
So now, this is not representative of all model

90
00:04:45,650 --> 00:04:50,410
usage, but it gives you a sense of what people on OpenRouter

91
00:04:50,410 --> 00:04:51,450
are doing.

92
00:04:51,450 --> 00:04:53,840
And so this is kind of more of an economic lens.

93
00:04:53,840 --> 00:04:58,000
I don't know what good is, but if people are paying for it,

94
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:01,660
it must be good.

95
00:05:01,660 --> 00:05:02,240
OK.

96
00:05:02,240 --> 00:05:07,200
So none of those are necessarily the correct answer.

97
00:05:07,200 --> 00:05:09,760
I don't know if there is a correct answer,

98
00:05:09,760 --> 00:05:12,820
but I'm just trying to get you to think about different ways

99
00:05:12,820 --> 00:05:16,060
of thinking about what good is.

100
00:05:16,060 --> 00:05:21,180
So let's start now by looking at different ways, concrete ways,

101
00:05:21,180 --> 00:05:23,300
of evaluating language models.

102
00:05:23,300 --> 00:05:26,460
And there's no better place to start than going back

103
00:05:26,460 --> 00:05:30,860
to the core idea of a language model, which

104
00:05:30,860 --> 00:05:36,180
is that it's a distribution P of x over sequences of tokens.

105
00:05:36,180 --> 00:05:38,620
And through that lens, there's a very natural way

106
00:05:38,620 --> 00:05:41,260
to evaluate a distribution, which

107
00:05:41,260 --> 00:05:46,540
is perplexity, or likelihood or log loss.

108
00:05:46,540 --> 00:05:49,540
Those are all kind of related concepts.

109
00:05:49,540 --> 00:05:54,240
And it basically says, I have a test data set d,

110
00:05:54,240 --> 00:05:58,680
and I'm going to see how much probability mass my language

111
00:05:58,680 --> 00:06:00,920
model assigns to that d.

112
00:06:00,920 --> 00:06:03,360
And I normalize it and divide so that the numbers

113
00:06:03,360 --> 00:06:05,840
are more interpretable.

114
00:06:05,840 --> 00:06:07,420
But that's basically it.

115
00:06:07,420 --> 00:06:11,720
How much mass does my probability language model

116
00:06:11,720 --> 00:06:14,440
assign to a data set.

117
00:06:14,440 --> 00:06:16,100
OK, so this is fairly natural.

118
00:06:16,100 --> 00:06:19,040
In fact, when we were doing training,

119
00:06:19,040 --> 00:06:22,600
we minimized the perplexity on the training set.

120
00:06:22,600 --> 00:06:25,400
So the obvious thing to do is measure the perplexity

121
00:06:25,400 --> 00:06:28,320
on the test set, and this is what people traditionally did

122
00:06:28,320 --> 00:06:31,280
in language modeling research for many years throughout

123
00:06:31,280 --> 00:06:33,240
the 2010s.

124
00:06:33,240 --> 00:06:35,380
If you pick up a language modeling paper,

125
00:06:35,380 --> 00:06:36,640
this is what they did.

126
00:06:36,640 --> 00:06:39,840
And back then, standard data sets

127
00:06:39,840 --> 00:06:44,320
included the Penn Treebank, WikiText-103.

128
00:06:44,320 --> 00:06:47,200
There was also this One Billion Word Benchmark,

129
00:06:47,200 --> 00:06:53,330
which was taken from machine translation sources.

130
00:06:53,330 --> 00:06:56,650
And this is all in the classic paradigm, which is

131
00:06:56,650 --> 00:06:58,750
in-distribution evaluation.

132
00:06:58,750 --> 00:07:02,110
You train on some train split of that data set,

133
00:07:02,110 --> 00:07:07,090
and then you evaluate on some test split of that data set.

134
00:07:07,090 --> 00:07:11,490
And people actually made a lot of progress,

135
00:07:11,490 --> 00:07:14,450
which was just measured in perplexity reduction.

136
00:07:14,450 --> 00:07:16,490
So for example, there's this famous paper

137
00:07:16,490 --> 00:07:22,490
from 2016, which showed that you could

138
00:07:22,490 --> 00:07:27,450
apply CNNs and LSTMs to this One Billion Word Benchmark

139
00:07:27,450 --> 00:07:31,090
and get a huge perplexity reduction.

140
00:07:31,090 --> 00:07:33,110
And bear in mind, around that time,

141
00:07:33,110 --> 00:07:35,490
people were talking still about n-gram models and maybe

142
00:07:35,490 --> 00:07:37,650
hybrid models.

143
00:07:37,650 --> 00:07:40,890
And this was the first definitive result

144
00:07:40,890 --> 00:07:46,050
that showed just pure neural is clearly the way to go.

145
00:07:46,050 --> 00:07:48,280
OK, so life was very simple back then.

146
00:07:48,280 --> 00:07:49,640
You train on the train split.

147
00:07:49,640 --> 00:07:52,900
You test on a test split for perplexity.

148
00:07:52,900 --> 00:07:58,380
So then when GPT-2 came around in 2019 from OpenAI,

149
00:07:58,380 --> 00:08:01,540
they trained on the data set called

150
00:08:01,540 --> 00:08:04,620
WebText, which was 40 gigabytes of text websites

151
00:08:04,620 --> 00:08:07,140
that were linked from Reddit.

152
00:08:07,140 --> 00:08:10,920
And the way they evaluated was actually different.

153
00:08:10,920 --> 00:08:16,060
They evaluated zero-shot on standard data sets

154
00:08:16,060 --> 00:08:19,380
that everyone else was evaluating on.

155
00:08:19,380 --> 00:08:21,840
So this is out-of-distribution evaluation.

156
00:08:21,840 --> 00:08:25,820
So this is a table from their paper, which shows that as they

157
00:08:25,820 --> 00:08:27,400
train different models.

158
00:08:27,400 --> 00:08:30,700
So the biggest model is 1.5 billion parameters.

159
00:08:30,700 --> 00:08:36,460
And they were able to show quite a bit of improvement,

160
00:08:36,460 --> 00:08:39,440
especially on the small data sets.

161
00:08:39,440 --> 00:08:42,780
So for example, PTB, which is a tiny dataset.

162
00:08:42,780 --> 00:08:46,480
They were getting 35 perplexity compared to the state

163
00:08:46,480 --> 00:08:48,400
of the art, which is 46.

164
00:08:48,400 --> 00:08:50,820
Now, if you have enough in-distribution data,

165
00:08:50,820 --> 00:08:53,960
you don't quite do as well as the SOTA.

166
00:08:53,960 --> 00:08:58,120
But nonetheless, this is kind of impressive,

167
00:08:58,120 --> 00:09:02,200
given that this model was not trained on One Billion Word's

168
00:09:02,200 --> 00:09:04,480
dataset at all.

169
00:09:04,480 --> 00:09:05,120
I don't know.

170
00:09:05,120 --> 00:09:07,040
Maybe there was some overlap, because I

171
00:09:07,040 --> 00:09:14,320
don't know if they did a careful train-test decontamination.

172
00:09:14,320 --> 00:09:20,440
And so it ushered in a new paradigm

173
00:09:20,440 --> 00:09:22,920
of thinking about language models as not just

174
00:09:22,920 --> 00:09:27,040
the in-distribution setting, but how

175
00:09:27,040 --> 00:09:31,480
we wanted to train on a large dataset

176
00:09:31,480 --> 00:09:34,560
and evaluate on standard benchmarks, which nowadays I

177
00:09:34,560 --> 00:09:37,300
think has been very common standard.

178
00:09:37,300 --> 00:09:41,620
But back then, it was seen as a bit of a novelty.

179
00:09:46,530 --> 00:09:49,890
I will also argue, since we're talking about perplexity, that

180
00:09:49,890 --> 00:09:53,050
perplexity is all you need.

181
00:09:53,050 --> 00:09:54,990
I wouldn't take this too seriously,

182
00:09:54,990 --> 00:09:57,250
but I think it is a mindset, I think,

183
00:09:57,250 --> 00:10:01,130
that drives a lot of language modeling research.

184
00:10:01,130 --> 00:10:03,810
And the basic argument goes like this.

185
00:10:03,810 --> 00:10:05,830
So there's some true distribution out there--

186
00:10:05,830 --> 00:10:09,970
let's call it t--and you're training this model p.

187
00:10:09,970 --> 00:10:14,210
So what is the best perplexity you could obtain?

188
00:10:14,210 --> 00:10:17,890
Well, the best perplexity is the entropy

189
00:10:17,890 --> 00:10:21,850
of that true distribution, and that

190
00:10:21,850 --> 00:10:26,130
is realized when p equals t.

191
00:10:26,130 --> 00:10:30,290
So by minimizing perplexity, you're basically

192
00:10:30,290 --> 00:10:35,130
pushing down the perplexity.

193
00:10:35,130 --> 00:10:38,610
And the only unique minimizer is when you actually

194
00:10:38,610 --> 00:10:40,780
get the true distribution.

195
00:10:40,780 --> 00:10:44,880
And when you have the true distribution, you're done.

196
00:10:44,880 --> 00:10:46,066
You just model everything.

197
00:10:46,066 --> 00:10:48,060
You can solve all the problems, and you

198
00:10:48,060 --> 00:10:50,780
can do things like condition on the problem,

199
00:10:50,780 --> 00:10:52,000
generate a solution.

200
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:55,740
You can condition on a question, generate the answer.

201
00:10:55,740 --> 00:10:59,140
所以通过降低困惑度，

202
00:10:59,140 --> 00:11:03,780
我们最终会达到AGI，这就是那个论点。

203
00:11:03,780 --> 00:11:05,500
我的意思是，这听起来可能，我不知道，

204
00:11:05,500 --> 00:11:08,100
要么有点天方夜谭。

205
00:11:08,100 --> 00:11:10,900
但从某种意义上说，这确实是，

206
00:11:10,900 --> 00:11:13,260
我认为，是许多人强有力的驱动力，

207
00:11:13,260 --> 00:11:16,980
他们在不断扩展语言模型，

208
00:11:16,980 --> 00:11:22,620
在那个收益也许不那么明显的时期。

209
00:11:22,620 --> 00:11:24,500
在GPT-3之前，我认为并不清楚

210
00:11:24,500 --> 00:11:29,180
语言模型会产生如此巨大的影响。

211
00:11:29,180 --> 00:11:33,020
而正是这种信念，如果你把困惑度

212
00:11:33,020 --> 00:11:36,940
降下来，好事就会发生。

213
00:11:36,940 --> 00:11:43,760
所以当然，困惑度也许并不是全部。

214
00:11:43,760 --> 00:11:47,760
而一种框架方式是，困惑度也许比

215
00:11:47,760 --> 00:11:49,025
你需要的更多。

216
00:11:49,025 --> 00:11:51,400
所以例如，如果你有一个句子像“Stanford was

217
00:11:51,400 --> 00:11:55,360
founded in 1885，”困惑度会说，

218
00:11:55,360 --> 00:11:59,400
惩罚所有token上的预测。

219
00:11:59,400 --> 00:12:03,640
所以特别是，它会说，1885的概率是多少，

220
00:12:03,640 --> 00:12:04,920
给定其余部分？

221
00:12:04,920 --> 00:12:07,980
而那个token可能相当有用。

222
00:12:07,980 --> 00:12:13,960
它基本上是一个问答例子，以句子补全的形式表达。

223
00:12:13,960 --> 00:12:17,480
所以它捕捉了关于世界的知识。

224
00:12:17,480 --> 00:12:22,180
但其他的——例如，句子的第一个词，

225
00:12:22,180 --> 00:12:29,560
甚至“founded”——也许没那么有趣，我认为。

226
00:12:29,560 --> 00:12:31,340
而困惑度并不在意。

227
00:12:31,340 --> 00:12:38,250
它会为每一点偏差向你收取比特，

228
00:12:38,250 --> 00:12:41,490
偏离真实分布。

229
00:12:41,490 --> 00:12:44,150
所以这里有一点解决方案，

230
00:12:44,150 --> 00:12:47,190
就是你可以总是测量条件困惑度。

231
00:12:47,190 --> 00:12:48,710
你可以以某个提示词为条件，

232
00:12:48,710 --> 00:12:53,530
然后你可以测量其余部分的困惑度

233
00:12:53,530 --> 00:12:54,450
响应。

234
00:12:54,450 --> 00:12:56,410
所以这允许你，一般来说，

235
00:12:56,410 --> 00:12:58,690
专注于某些你相信

236
00:12:58,690 --> 00:13:02,730
相关的token，而较少关注其他

237
00:13:02,730 --> 00:13:06,590
那些是附带性的。

238
00:13:06,590 --> 00:13:08,916
好的。

239
00:13:08,916 --> 00:13:11,570
而且，重要的是要记住

240
00:13:11,570 --> 00:13:15,570
一些基准测试，即使它们没有明确

241
00:13:15,570 --> 00:13:17,130
a perplexity measure, are actually

242
00:13:17,130 --> 00:13:18,930
just perplexity in disguise.

243
00:13:18,930 --> 00:13:22,110
So, for example, if you think about closed tasks.

244
00:13:22,110 --> 00:13:26,110
There's this LAMBADA paper from, actually, a while ago,

245
00:13:26,110 --> 00:13:29,650
2016, which is basically fill in the blank.

246
00:13:29,650 --> 00:13:33,290
And you have a context, you have a sentence.

247
00:13:33,290 --> 00:13:36,380
"Do you honestly think I will want you to have a blank."

248
00:13:36,380 --> 00:13:40,260
And the goal is to figure out that word.

249
00:13:40,260 --> 00:13:44,320
And even though this is measured in terms of accuracy,

250
00:13:44,320 --> 00:13:48,900
it is really a next-token prediction problem.

251
00:13:48,900 --> 00:13:52,680
And here, it's not perplexity of all the words,

252
00:13:52,680 --> 00:13:54,000
it's carefully chosen.

253
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:56,940
If you read this paper, it's really carefully chosen

254
00:13:56,940 --> 00:14:00,060
to be words, such that you actually need

255
00:14:00,060 --> 00:14:03,780
a bunch of long contexts to resolve.

256
00:14:03,780 --> 00:14:08,980
So this is actually something that the early GPT papers really

257
00:14:08,980 --> 00:14:14,820
latched on to because they were interested in long-context

258
00:14:14,820 --> 00:14:17,980
modeling, thinking that that was a key to unlocking

259
00:14:17,980 --> 00:14:20,900
reasoning and all these things.

260
00:14:20,900 --> 00:14:24,620
And really, by focusing on positions

261
00:14:24,620 --> 00:14:29,300
where, to resolve that, it requires

262
00:14:29,300 --> 00:14:34,870
long-distance dependencies, is a way

263
00:14:34,870 --> 00:14:39,350
to sharpen the perplexity to some phenomenon

264
00:14:39,350 --> 00:14:41,910
that you care about.

265
00:14:41,910 --> 00:14:44,270
Here's another popular classic data

266
00:14:44,270 --> 00:14:46,890
set that you see in some language modeling vowels.

267
00:14:46,890 --> 00:14:47,990
HellaSwag.

268
00:14:47,990 --> 00:14:49,170
So what is HellaSwag?

269
00:14:49,170 --> 00:14:52,950
They went to various different data sets and sources,

270
00:14:52,950 --> 00:14:56,030
and came up with-- it's multiple choice,

271
00:14:56,030 --> 00:14:57,850
but it's really sentence completion.

272
00:14:57,850 --> 00:15:00,130
And at some level, it's perplexity.

273
00:15:00,130 --> 00:15:01,550
So here's a sentence.

274
00:15:01,550 --> 00:15:03,410
"A woman is outside with a bucket and a dog.

275
00:15:03,410 --> 00:15:04,690
The dog is running around.

276
00:15:04,690 --> 00:15:05,805
She blank."

277
00:15:05,805 --> 00:15:07,430
And then you're trying to fill in which

278
00:15:07,430 --> 00:15:10,150
sentence completes it the best.

279
00:15:10,150 --> 00:15:12,650
So it's not exactly perplexity, it's multiple choice.

280
00:15:12,650 --> 00:15:18,910
But in some ways, it's perplexity.

281
00:15:18,910 --> 00:15:20,970
So one word of warning.

282
00:15:20,970 --> 00:15:25,670
If you ever decide that you're sold on perplexity

283
00:15:25,670 --> 00:15:29,070
as, really, the measure, and you're

284
00:15:29,070 --> 00:15:32,170
going to launch a leaderboard and have people compete

285
00:15:32,170 --> 00:15:35,890
against perplexity, just a word of caution

286
00:15:35,890 --> 00:15:39,810
is that, what does a perplexity leaderboard look like?

287
00:15:39,810 --> 00:15:43,130
So people are going to submit a language model to you,

288
00:15:43,130 --> 00:15:47,230
and you have to compute a logprob.

289
00:15:47,230 --> 00:15:51,050
You send them the test data, and they give you back a logprob.

290
00:15:51,050 --> 00:15:53,130
Well, you look at that and say, hmm,

291
00:15:53,130 --> 00:15:58,130
that might be a little bit fishy because you

292
00:15:58,130 --> 00:16:01,650
need to trust that the probabilities they hand back

293
00:16:01,650 --> 00:16:06,390
to you are valid and that they sum to 1.

294
00:16:06,390 --> 00:16:08,490
Otherwise, I could just implement LM,

295
00:16:08,490 --> 00:16:14,410
and it always returns 1 or, I guess, logprob 0.

296
00:16:14,410 --> 00:16:17,270
And I would get very good perplexity,

297
00:16:17,270 --> 00:16:22,530
but clearly, that's not a valid distribution.

298
00:16:22,530 --> 00:16:24,210
And so with this contract, it's not

299
00:16:24,210 --> 00:16:27,650
really easy to check that someone is actually

300
00:16:27,650 --> 00:16:30,520
internally having a log probability, so there's some trust.

301
00:16:30,520 --> 00:16:33,860
Maybe you have to look at the code or something.

302
00:16:33,860 --> 00:16:39,060
For downstream tasks, this is much more straightforward

303
00:16:39,060 --> 00:16:40,707
because you can have a black box LM.

304
00:16:40,707 --> 00:16:42,540
I give you a prompt, you give me a response,

305
00:16:42,540 --> 00:16:46,260
and I can just use that response and calculate some accuracy.

306
00:16:46,260 --> 00:16:48,140
So perplexity is fundamentally different

307
00:16:48,140 --> 00:16:50,080
because it deals with distributions,

308
00:16:50,080 --> 00:16:52,660
and you have to be a bit careful.

309
00:16:52,660 --> 00:16:55,980
And not to mention, there's lots of problems

310
00:16:55,980 --> 00:16:57,200
with autoregressive models.

311
00:16:57,200 --> 00:17:01,180
But if you have VAEs or there's some models where you can't even

312
00:17:01,180 --> 00:17:04,780
compute the distribution—you can compute only a bound—

313
00:17:04,780 --> 00:17:11,060
then you have to really actually trust the math as well that this is actually a valid bound.

314
00:17:11,060 --> 00:17:12,560
OK.

315
00:17:12,560 --> 00:17:16,420
So the summary of perplexity is that it is actually

316
00:17:16,420 --> 00:17:22,819
still used quite heavily in language model development.

317
00:17:22,819 --> 00:17:24,280
It has some nice properties.

318
00:17:24,280 --> 00:17:27,619
We saw that it's generally used for scaling laws

319
00:17:27,619 --> 00:17:30,910
because it's also smoothly varying

320
00:17:30,910 --> 00:17:35,510
with scale, which is important for getting scaling laws.

321
00:17:35,510 --> 00:17:40,190
But, at the end of the day, especially if you're not

322
00:17:40,190 --> 00:17:46,990
a believer, then you need some benchmarks to really capture real world situations, to convince people

323
00:17:46,990 --> 00:17:48,990
that your language model is good.

324
00:17:48,990 --> 00:17:53,090
For the believers, if I show you a remarkably low perplexity,

325
00:17:53,090 --> 00:17:56,270
they'll be convinced.

326
00:17:56,270 --> 00:17:58,530
OK, any questions about perplexity?

327
00:18:10,370 --> 00:18:10,870
OK.

328
00:18:10,870 --> 00:18:14,550
Hopefully, everyone's a believer now.

329
00:18:14,550 --> 00:18:18,590
OK, so let's talk about exam benchmarks.

330
00:18:18,590 --> 00:18:21,830
So exams, as all of you students know,

331
00:18:21,830 --> 00:18:26,130
is a way that we use to test humans.

332
00:18:26,130 --> 00:18:28,330
And so you can adopt the same mentality

333
00:18:28,330 --> 00:18:30,730
to test language models.

334
00:18:30,730 --> 00:18:32,370
The nice thing about exams is that you

335
00:18:32,370 --> 00:18:35,570
have a very careful control over the subject,

336
00:18:35,570 --> 00:18:38,410
over the difficulty.

337
00:18:38,410 --> 00:18:42,510
You can design them to have unambiguous correct answers,

338
00:18:42,510 --> 00:18:44,650
which makes them easy to grade.

339
00:18:44,650 --> 00:18:47,930
So there's a lot of nice properties about exams.

340
00:18:47,930 --> 00:18:52,410
And so, for this reason, a lot of LM benchmarking culture

341
00:18:52,410 --> 00:18:56,210
was based on just this idea of exams.

342
00:18:56,210 --> 00:18:59,783
So obviously, there's been a lot of benchmarks,

343
00:18:59,783 --> 00:19:01,450
but I'll just highlight some of the ones

344
00:19:01,450 --> 00:19:04,850
that I think have been influential

345
00:19:04,850 --> 00:19:07,290
in the development of language models.

346
00:19:07,290 --> 00:19:11,450
So this one is MMLU, or Massive Multitask Language Understanding

347
00:19:11,450 --> 00:19:15,770
from Hendrycks et al, 2020.

348
00:19:15,770 --> 00:19:19,010
And at that time, it wasn't clear

349
00:19:19,010 --> 00:19:23,690
that language models were really like general purpose task

350
00:19:23,690 --> 00:19:24,190
solvers.

351
00:19:24,190 --> 00:19:29,460
This was around the time when GPT-3 came out.

352
00:19:29,460 --> 00:19:31,780
Many people, I think, still thought of language models

353
00:19:31,780 --> 00:19:33,800
as, well, language models.

354
00:19:33,800 --> 00:19:36,340
You generate fluent English.

355
00:19:36,340 --> 00:19:38,940
But they had this idea that, well,

356
00:19:38,940 --> 00:19:41,880
let's just try to be ahead of the curve here.

357
00:19:41,880 --> 00:19:46,040
We're going to go through 57 subjects,

358
00:19:46,040 --> 00:19:50,140
we're going to have a team of students

359
00:19:50,140 --> 00:19:52,900
scour the internet for different sources,

360
00:19:52,900 --> 00:20:00,020
and really put together this very comprehensive data set.

361
00:20:00,020 --> 00:20:03,420
And despite the name MMLU, which has the word Language

362
00:20:03,420 --> 00:20:05,180
Understanding, it's really about testing

363
00:20:05,180 --> 00:20:07,580
knowledge and reasoning and less so just

364
00:20:07,580 --> 00:20:09,660
pure language understanding.

365
00:20:09,660 --> 00:20:14,020
And the way they evaluated it was with GPT-3,

366
00:20:14,020 --> 00:20:15,720
using few-shot prompting.

367
00:20:15,720 --> 00:20:22,140
So they essentially said, I have formed this prompt.

368
00:20:22,140 --> 00:20:24,890
The following are questions about high school mathematics.

369
00:20:24,890 --> 00:20:28,390
Some in-context examples of question, answer, question,

370
00:20:28,390 --> 00:20:31,510
answer, and then the final question, and then the model

371
00:20:31,510 --> 00:20:33,830
has to predict the answer.

372
00:20:33,830 --> 00:20:35,650
So this seems very mundane.

373
00:20:35,650 --> 00:20:38,070
But at that time it was, I think,

374
00:20:38,070 --> 00:20:41,630
pretty radical that you would construct

375
00:20:41,630 --> 00:20:46,270
this fairly complicated question and with in-context examples

376
00:20:46,270 --> 00:20:48,070
and everything, and expect a language

377
00:20:48,070 --> 00:20:51,830
model to actually do something reasonable.

378
00:20:51,830 --> 00:20:57,970
And sure enough, at the outset, the models were not great,

379
00:20:57,970 --> 00:20:58,550
I would say.

380
00:21:01,430 --> 00:21:04,890
Some of the small models were barely above chance.

381
00:21:04,890 --> 00:21:07,470
And the larger model-- this GPT-3--

382
00:21:07,470 --> 00:21:13,910
was actually well above chance, so this was kind of encouraging.

383
00:21:13,910 --> 00:21:21,930
And then over the years, as we know,

384
00:21:21,930 --> 00:21:26,030
this benchmark has essentially saturated.

385
00:21:26,030 --> 00:21:29,550
So this is a site I don't think this has all the data in it,

386
00:21:29,550 --> 00:21:32,110
but about 3.5 Turbo.

387
00:21:32,110 --> 00:21:35,290
This was like--

388
00:21:35,290 --> 00:21:41,090
I guess, beginning of 2024-- oh, '23 actually--to GPT-4.

389
00:21:41,090 --> 00:21:48,030
And then, now it's in the 90s.

390
00:21:48,030 --> 00:21:52,250
So there's also a link you can follow up

391
00:21:52,250 --> 00:21:54,210
later on, where you can actually browse some

392
00:21:54,210 --> 00:21:57,610
of the predictions of these models, which is always

393
00:21:57,610 --> 00:22:00,690
fun to see what models actually goof up on

394
00:22:00,690 --> 00:22:03,850
and what they get right.

395
00:22:03,850 --> 00:22:06,355
OK.

396
00:22:06,355 --> 00:22:10,930
So around 2024, when it seemed like MMLU, there

397
00:22:10,930 --> 00:22:13,757
was a lot of progress made, it was like, OK--

398
00:22:13,757 --> 00:22:15,340
you're going to see this theme again--

399
00:22:15,340 --> 00:22:18,570
OK, this benchmark is getting too easy.

400
00:22:18,570 --> 00:22:22,525
Let's try to make it harder.

401
00:22:22,525 --> 00:22:23,900
Well, first of all, they actually

402
00:22:23,900 --> 00:22:26,320
noticed that there were some noisy and trivial problems,

403
00:22:26,320 --> 00:22:27,900
so let's get rid of them.

404
00:22:27,900 --> 00:22:30,960
Four choices might be a little bit too easy,

405
00:22:30,960 --> 00:22:34,300
so let's expand that to 10.

406
00:22:34,300 --> 00:22:38,820
At that point, it was much more in vogue to use chain of thought

407
00:22:38,820 --> 00:22:42,740
to solve these benchmarks, whereas when GPT-3 came out,

408
00:22:42,740 --> 00:22:46,300
that wasn't really a thing.

409
00:22:46,300 --> 00:22:48,820
And it's clear that some of these questions

410
00:22:48,820 --> 00:22:52,060
could benefit from a few reasoning steps.

411
00:22:52,060 --> 00:22:55,740
So this new benchmark was harder.

412
00:22:55,740 --> 00:22:59,820
So now the accuracy of the model is back at 33.

413
00:22:59,820 --> 00:23:04,140
But as you can maybe see from the progress

414
00:23:04,140 --> 00:23:07,600
so far it started low.

415
00:23:07,600 --> 00:23:13,200
And now it's back to, I guess it's like 88, almost 90, OK.

416
00:23:13,200 --> 00:23:18,350
So this benchmark might be a little bit too easy as well.

417
00:23:18,350 --> 00:23:20,830
OK, so there's another benchmark called

418
00:23:20,830 --> 00:23:26,790
GPQA, which stands for Google-Proof QA.

419
00:23:26,790 --> 00:23:31,950
And the idea here is that, OK, how can we

420
00:23:31,950 --> 00:23:35,930
get a really good benchmark?

421
00:23:35,930 --> 00:23:38,270
And the idea is that, well, if you can solve a problem

422
00:23:38,270 --> 00:23:39,710
by looking at Google.

423
00:23:39,710 --> 00:23:42,230
Google has the internet and a language model

424
00:23:42,230 --> 00:23:43,370
is trained on the internet.

425
00:23:43,370 --> 00:23:46,410
So if you can just solve it, then that's probably too easy.

426
00:23:46,410 --> 00:23:49,970
So they got some now PhD level.

427
00:23:49,970 --> 00:23:55,910
So I think MMLU was thought about as actually--

428
00:23:55,910 --> 00:24:00,050
maybe some of these were like undergrad but maybe grad level.

429
00:24:00,050 --> 00:24:03,110
But now, it's more explicitly about questions that

430
00:24:03,110 --> 00:24:06,310
are meant to be pretty hard.

431
00:24:06,310 --> 00:24:08,897
And at this point now you say, OK,

432
00:24:08,897 --> 00:24:10,730
how do I come up with really hard questions?

433
00:24:10,730 --> 00:24:13,790
You actually have to go through quite a bit of human labor

434
00:24:13,790 --> 00:24:15,960
to get really hard questions.

435
00:24:15,960 --> 00:24:19,920
So this was 61 PhD contractors from Upwork.

436
00:24:19,920 --> 00:24:21,680
Someone writes a question.

437
00:24:21,680 --> 00:24:24,680
It goes through an expert validation which then provides

438
00:24:24,680 --> 00:24:26,920
feedback on the questions.

439
00:24:26,920 --> 00:24:29,740
And then the question writer goes and revises the question.

440
00:24:29,740 --> 00:24:32,360
And another expert looks at the question.

441
00:24:32,360 --> 00:24:36,340
And then you have the question.

442
00:24:36,340 --> 00:24:38,360
And then you send it to some non-experts

443
00:24:38,360 --> 00:24:41,220
who are given Google access.

444
00:24:41,220 --> 00:24:44,120
And then you see if they can solve the question.

445
00:24:44,120 --> 00:24:47,320
So there's a subset of these questions called the DIAMOND set

446
00:24:47,320 --> 00:24:51,800
that you might see in people's leaderboards

447
00:24:51,800 --> 00:24:56,000
where if the two experts agree and one of the non-experts

448
00:24:56,000 --> 00:24:59,280
is able to answer the question, then that's deemed a good kind

449
00:24:59,280 --> 00:25:01,240
of DIAMOND question.

450
00:25:01,240 --> 00:25:05,160
So this is a pretty rigorous review process.

451
00:25:05,160 --> 00:25:12,300
And PhD experts were only able to get 65%.

452
00:25:12,300 --> 00:25:15,680
So this is I think mostly four-way multiple choice.

453
00:25:15,680 --> 00:25:18,280
So this is above random.

454
00:25:18,280 --> 00:25:25,260
But this is not a good grade if you're getting 65% on an exam.

455
00:25:25,260 --> 00:25:28,900
And it's not experts, even if they had 30 minutes with access

456
00:25:28,900 --> 00:25:33,860
to Google, they were getting a bit over chance, but not much.

457
00:25:33,860 --> 00:25:37,820
And at that point GPT-4 was like 39%,

458
00:25:37,820 --> 00:25:40,960
and let's see how it's doing now.

459
00:25:45,140 --> 00:25:54,000
So GPQA is now again 94.

460
00:25:54,000 --> 00:26:01,692
So these benchmarks have a shelf life of not too high I guess.

461
00:26:01,692 --> 00:26:03,400
I will say that some of these benchmarks,

462
00:26:03,400 --> 00:26:05,520
even though they saturate the larger models,

463
00:26:05,520 --> 00:26:07,060
they're still useful for developing

464
00:26:07,060 --> 00:26:10,940
smaller models or fitting scaling laws and so on.

465
00:26:10,940 --> 00:26:14,950
So then, yeah.

466
00:26:14,950 --> 00:26:18,670
How do you know that when these tests are

467
00:26:18,670 --> 00:26:22,390
being used for training and actually your models

468
00:26:22,390 --> 00:26:24,270
are communicating with these?

469
00:26:24,270 --> 00:26:26,690
Yeah, so this is a very important question.

470
00:26:26,690 --> 00:26:29,750
So how do you know that these questions aren't

471
00:26:29,750 --> 00:26:32,390
just in the training set?

472
00:26:32,390 --> 00:26:35,270
So the short answer is we don't know because I don't know

473
00:26:35,270 --> 00:26:37,150
what's in the training set.

474
00:26:37,150 --> 00:26:39,030
I'll come back to this contamination

475
00:26:39,030 --> 00:26:42,530
point a little bit later and ways of addressing it.

476
00:26:42,530 --> 00:26:45,330
But you should always--I'm glad you asked this.

477
00:26:45,330 --> 00:26:46,990
You should always take these numbers

478
00:26:46,990 --> 00:26:51,170
with a little bit of a grain of salt, because it's only trust.

479
00:26:54,590 --> 00:26:55,970
Maybe just to say one more.

480
00:26:55,970 --> 00:26:59,870
I think train contamination is a little bit subtle.

481
00:26:59,870 --> 00:27:01,830
Because it's probably not the case that

482
00:27:01,830 --> 00:27:04,310
they literally train on the set.

483
00:27:04,310 --> 00:27:08,050
But questions can be derived from other sources.

484
00:27:08,050 --> 00:27:09,760
And those sources could be trained on.

485
00:27:09,760 --> 00:27:14,720
So often contamination is a much more subtle process

486
00:27:14,720 --> 00:27:19,440
than literally accidentally training on the test.

487
00:27:19,440 --> 00:27:20,700
Was there another question?

488
00:27:20,700 --> 00:27:21,480
Same question.

489
00:27:21,480 --> 00:27:23,280
OK, great.

490
00:27:23,280 --> 00:27:25,800
Good, always good to be skeptical.

491
00:27:25,800 --> 00:27:29,320
OK, so the final exam benchmark I'll talk about

492
00:27:29,320 --> 00:27:34,400
is something ominously called Humanity's Last Exam.

493
00:27:34,400 --> 00:27:36,480
This is from last year.

494
00:27:36,480 --> 00:27:38,320
And they were saying, OK, well these models

495
00:27:38,320 --> 00:27:39,500
are getting so good.

496
00:27:39,500 --> 00:27:41,560
Let's just give it all we can to create

497
00:27:41,560 --> 00:27:44,080
something that's really tough.

498
00:27:44,080 --> 00:27:46,460
So this is multimodal, many subjects.

499
00:27:46,460 --> 00:27:47,620
It's still multiple choice.

500
00:27:47,620 --> 00:27:50,200
And I guess some short answer.

501
00:27:50,200 --> 00:27:53,340
So this was crowdsourced.

502
00:27:53,340 --> 00:27:59,120
They had some money to incentivize people who were

503
00:27:59,120 --> 00:28:01,460
into money to create questions.

504
00:28:01,460 --> 00:28:03,240
They also incentivize with authorship

505
00:28:03,240 --> 00:28:06,160
for those who are into that.

506
00:28:06,160 --> 00:28:08,740
And they went through multiple stages of reviews

507
00:28:08,740 --> 00:28:10,860
filtered by frontier models.

508
00:28:10,860 --> 00:28:12,540
There's a whole process.

509
00:28:12,540 --> 00:28:15,780
Another answer to your question is that they also held out

510
00:28:15,780 --> 00:28:18,480
a private set that was not released publicly,

511
00:28:18,480 --> 00:28:22,340
so that they could make sure that this did not

512
00:28:22,340 --> 00:28:24,260
enter training.

513
00:28:24,260 --> 00:28:26,220
And then you just hope that the APIs

514
00:28:26,220 --> 00:28:29,440
that you-- because you still have to send this to the model,

515
00:28:29,440 --> 00:28:32,740
that those prompts don't end up in the training.

516
00:28:32,740 --> 00:28:36,740
But let's hope that's the case.

517
00:28:36,740 --> 00:28:41,640
So every data set paper looks like this.

518
00:28:41,640 --> 00:28:44,640
Previous benchmarks, look the models are doing so well.

519
00:28:44,640 --> 00:28:48,460
My data set, look all the models are doing really poorly.

520
00:28:48,460 --> 00:28:50,320
So this is a common pattern.

521
00:28:50,320 --> 00:28:52,200
And this was especially in single digits.

522
00:28:52,200 --> 00:28:55,620
It's pretty abysmal here.

523
00:28:55,620 --> 00:29:01,020
And let's see how we're doing in 2026.

524
00:29:01,020 --> 00:29:05,680
So this one is-- it still has some room.

525
00:29:05,680 --> 00:29:11,670
So it's only at-- even Mythos is only at 64.7.

526
00:29:11,670 --> 00:29:16,050
So this is indeed a pretty-- it seems like a pretty hard exam.

527
00:29:20,550 --> 00:29:26,230
OK, so to summarize this section, as you can see,

528
00:29:26,230 --> 00:29:28,470
we've trained it towards harder and harder questions

529
00:29:28,470 --> 00:29:33,030
as the models improve and we saturate existing benchmarks.

530
00:29:33,030 --> 00:29:37,230
It's interesting that multiple choice format still survives

531
00:29:37,230 --> 00:29:41,470
because it's-- with multiple choice, I mean, you

532
00:29:41,470 --> 00:29:44,030
can make it as difficult as you want.

533
00:29:44,030 --> 00:29:47,130
So some people think, well, multiple choice is too easy.

534
00:29:47,130 --> 00:29:48,990
Well, I mean, I can give you a very hard

535
00:29:48,990 --> 00:29:51,010
multiple-choice question.

536
00:29:51,010 --> 00:29:53,630
That's not really the point.

537
00:29:53,630 --> 00:29:56,670
I think more the point is that multiple choice restricts

538
00:29:56,670 --> 00:29:58,790
the set of questions you can ask.

539
00:29:58,790 --> 00:30:03,510
But in terms of difficulty, no problem with multiple choice.

540
00:30:03,510 --> 00:30:07,880
The main problem with exam-based questions

541
00:30:07,880 --> 00:30:12,400
is that this does not capture real-world usage.

542
00:30:12,400 --> 00:30:17,160
No one asks HLE questions to a language model

543
00:30:17,160 --> 00:30:20,440
except for when you're evaluating on HLE.

544
00:30:20,440 --> 00:30:24,160
Most of the time people are asking open-ended questions.

545
00:30:24,160 --> 00:30:26,620
They don't even necessarily have a correct answer.

546
00:30:26,620 --> 00:30:29,800
They might not be well formed, and these benchmarks

547
00:30:29,800 --> 00:30:33,080
do not capture that.

548
00:30:33,080 --> 00:30:37,077
OK, so question.

549
00:30:37,077 --> 00:30:39,660
How is the model output compared with the ground truth answer?

550
00:30:39,660 --> 00:30:42,520
So for HLE?

551
00:30:42,520 --> 00:30:44,360
For accuracy, how is the model's output

552
00:30:44,360 --> 00:30:47,000
compared with the ground truth answer,

553
00:30:47,000 --> 00:30:48,400
to calculate the accuracy?

554
00:30:48,400 --> 00:30:51,880
To calculate the accuracy, how is the ground truth

555
00:30:51,880 --> 00:30:52,800
compared?

556
00:30:52,800 --> 00:30:56,640
So for multiple choice, it's just like,

557
00:30:56,640 --> 00:30:59,078
did you get the right answer?

558
00:30:59,078 --> 00:31:01,620
So will we check the probability distribution for each option

559
00:31:01,620 --> 00:31:09,330
or how is-- So the language model generates C.

560
00:31:09,330 --> 00:31:12,850
And my answer is either B or C or A or D,

561
00:31:12,850 --> 00:31:16,010
and I just check if they're the same or not.

562
00:31:16,010 --> 00:31:18,890
So we are only generating one token?

563
00:31:18,890 --> 00:31:19,990
Yes, well, OK.

564
00:31:19,990 --> 00:31:22,150
So then the question is less about evaluation.

565
00:31:22,150 --> 00:31:25,250
But how does the model generate an answer?

566
00:31:25,250 --> 00:31:27,710
So there's a few options.

567
00:31:27,710 --> 00:31:30,010
One is that you generate--

568
00:31:30,010 --> 00:31:32,450
literally you sample and it generates a letter.

569
00:31:32,450 --> 00:31:35,670
Or more commonly now you generate a chain of thought.

570
00:31:35,670 --> 00:31:38,450
You generate the answer maybe some explanation.

571
00:31:38,450 --> 00:31:43,690
But there is a way of extracting an answer which does matter.

572
00:31:43,690 --> 00:31:47,150
And language model evaluations can be very sensitive to that

573
00:31:47,150 --> 00:31:49,230
but I'm not going to talk about that here.

574
00:31:52,370 --> 00:31:56,890
So let's talk about a very different class of benchmarks

575
00:31:56,890 --> 00:32:00,490
called chat benchmarks.

576
00:32:00,490 --> 00:32:03,850
So far we've talked about multiple-choice tasks.

577
00:32:03,850 --> 00:32:07,190
So they're still pretty good at capturing some notion

578
00:32:07,190 --> 00:32:09,950
of intelligence and difficulty.

579
00:32:09,950 --> 00:32:13,630
But most people don't ask a multiple-choice exam question

580
00:32:13,630 --> 00:32:15,990
to their AI assistant unless they're

581
00:32:15,990 --> 00:32:19,530
trying to use their AI system to do their exam for them,

582
00:32:19,530 --> 00:32:21,270
which you would never.

583
00:32:21,270 --> 00:32:23,150
One does that.

584
00:32:23,150 --> 00:32:29,150
OK, so here's an example semi-inspired by a real world

585
00:32:29,150 --> 00:32:30,110
use case.

586
00:32:30,110 --> 00:32:33,650
So I might say I want to make a beet salad with cheese.

587
00:32:33,650 --> 00:32:36,510
What herbs would work well and what wouldn't work well?

588
00:32:36,510 --> 00:32:39,170
OK, so this is a very open-ended question.

589
00:32:39,170 --> 00:32:42,810
And then you get some response, which is, again,

590
00:32:42,810 --> 00:32:45,350
an open-ended response.

591
00:32:45,350 --> 00:32:48,710
And then the question is, how do you evaluate this thing?

592
00:32:48,710 --> 00:32:51,370
You can't just check exact match with equality.

593
00:32:51,370 --> 00:32:54,310
There's no ground truth even.

594
00:32:54,310 --> 00:32:56,670
So there's a few ideas here.

595
00:32:56,670 --> 00:32:58,390
We can go through them.

596
00:32:58,390 --> 00:33:04,840
So one of the ideas was pioneered by this Chatbot Arena.

597
00:33:04,840 --> 00:33:09,640
And the idea is, well, you ask humans, OK.

598
00:33:09,640 --> 00:33:13,400
But the way they ask humans, I think is a clever idea.

599
00:33:13,400 --> 00:33:18,000
So they basically had this set of this website

600
00:33:18,000 --> 00:33:23,680
where any random person from the internet can go in and chat.

601
00:33:23,680 --> 00:33:27,080
And then instead of a normal assistant

602
00:33:27,080 --> 00:33:29,440
in getting one response, you actually

603
00:33:29,440 --> 00:33:33,680
get two responses from two different models

604
00:33:33,680 --> 00:33:38,120
which are anonymized, and then they rate which one is better.

605
00:33:38,120 --> 00:33:41,000
So for example, you put in this response,

606
00:33:41,000 --> 00:33:45,760
you get these two, assistant A, assistant B. And then you ask,

607
00:33:45,760 --> 00:33:49,920
A is better, both are good, both are bad, B is better?

608
00:33:49,920 --> 00:33:53,840
So using this process, you can get

609
00:33:53,840 --> 00:33:58,930
a bunch of pairwise comparison data of model A, model B, which

610
00:33:58,930 --> 00:34:00,330
one is better?

611
00:34:00,330 --> 00:34:03,730
And then you can use this to compute ELO rankings

612
00:34:03,730 --> 00:34:04,930
based on this.

613
00:34:04,930 --> 00:34:07,890
So I don't know how many of you are familiar with this

614
00:34:07,890 --> 00:34:11,690
but just get everyone on the same page.

615
00:34:11,690 --> 00:34:17,489
In these ELO rankings, you define your rating model

616
00:34:17,489 --> 00:34:20,610
as a probability of a particular model being

617
00:34:20,610 --> 00:34:27,090
another [INAUDIBLE] model as some function of the ELO

618
00:34:27,090 --> 00:34:34,889
scores of model B and model A. So the larger the ELO score

619
00:34:34,889 --> 00:34:37,590
of A is, the higher the probability.

620
00:34:37,590 --> 00:34:39,290
So it's a smooth mapping.

621
00:34:39,290 --> 00:34:42,909
And then you fit this model with the parameters,

622
00:34:42,909 --> 00:34:44,850
which are the ELO ratings, to maximize

623
00:34:44,850 --> 00:34:48,350
the probability of the pairwise comparisons, OK.

624
00:34:48,350 --> 00:34:49,929
And then as a result, you basically

625
00:34:49,929 --> 00:34:55,949
get a rating for each of your models.

626
00:34:55,949 --> 00:34:59,510
OK, so this is what Chatbot Arena, nowadays known

627
00:34:59,510 --> 00:35:02,230
as Arena AI, did.

628
00:35:02,230 --> 00:35:06,750
And you can get this nice ranking.

629
00:35:06,750 --> 00:35:10,950
I guess Claude Opus is doing pretty well here

630
00:35:10,950 --> 00:35:14,710
but some other models are on the list as well.

631
00:35:14,710 --> 00:35:15,950
OK.

632
00:35:15,950 --> 00:35:20,010
So there's a few things that are nice about this setup.

633
00:35:20,010 --> 00:35:24,310
One is that you have real-world prompts.

634
00:35:24,310 --> 00:35:27,148
So the idea is that anyone can come to the site,

635
00:35:27,148 --> 00:35:29,190
and the incentive for someone to come to the site

636
00:35:29,190 --> 00:35:32,430
is that they get free access to a language model.

637
00:35:32,430 --> 00:35:35,310
And so there's this implicit assumption

638
00:35:35,310 --> 00:35:39,330
that they're actually trying to use it to do something useful.

639
00:35:39,330 --> 00:35:47,630
And therefore you get a real-world prompt.

640
00:35:47,630 --> 00:35:52,110
But one thing you should keep in mind is, who are these people?

641
00:35:52,110 --> 00:35:54,240
Random person on the internet is--

642
00:35:54,240 --> 00:35:56,860
I don't know what that distribution.

643
00:35:56,860 --> 00:35:59,120
Random person on the internet who comes to La Marina.

644
00:35:59,120 --> 00:36:01,460
I don't know what that distribution looks like.

645
00:36:01,460 --> 00:36:03,900
I think in their paper, they have some demographics,

646
00:36:03,900 --> 00:36:07,320
but demographics don't tell the whole story.

647
00:36:07,320 --> 00:36:09,560
You could worry about different biases coming

648
00:36:09,560 --> 00:36:12,820
from the different models.

649
00:36:12,820 --> 00:36:13,860
Maybe spammers.

650
00:36:13,860 --> 00:36:19,520
People are trying to game, who maybe even submitted a model

651
00:36:19,520 --> 00:36:24,040
and they want their model to look good.

652
00:36:24,040 --> 00:36:25,460
You just don't know.

653
00:36:25,460 --> 00:36:29,080
It's a little bit of a Wild West.

654
00:36:29,080 --> 00:36:32,760
There's another issue which is that the binary preference,

655
00:36:32,760 --> 00:36:35,880
which is better, A or B is very nice and simple

656
00:36:35,880 --> 00:36:41,160
and feeds into this rating, but does conflate style

657
00:36:41,160 --> 00:36:43,160
and correctness.

658
00:36:43,160 --> 00:36:45,100
So ELO ratings for chess makes a lot of sense,

659
00:36:45,100 --> 00:36:47,360
because the only thing that matters is, did you win?

660
00:36:47,360 --> 00:36:54,810
But which one is better is much less clear cut.

661
00:36:54,810 --> 00:37:00,310
And there's questions of the human is the judge here.

662
00:37:00,310 --> 00:37:03,990
The person who puts in the prompt is answering the question.

663
00:37:03,990 --> 00:37:06,230
And this is good because in some level,

664
00:37:06,230 --> 00:37:10,810
because the person has some intent

665
00:37:10,810 --> 00:37:15,330
and they can declare whether their intent was met.

666
00:37:15,330 --> 00:37:16,938
To some extent, except for they're

667
00:37:16,938 --> 00:37:18,730
presumably asking the question because they

668
00:37:18,730 --> 00:37:19,670
don't know the answer.

669
00:37:19,670 --> 00:37:22,010
So if I give you two answers, how

670
00:37:22,010 --> 00:37:25,090
are they to judge which one is correct?

671
00:37:25,090 --> 00:37:27,630
And then there's also the problem of sycophancy,

672
00:37:27,630 --> 00:37:31,210
where maybe more pleasing answers

673
00:37:31,210 --> 00:37:34,570
are going to be upweighted highly compared

674
00:37:34,570 --> 00:37:37,770
to correct but honest answers.

675
00:37:37,770 --> 00:37:42,810
So there's a lot of potential issues here.

676
00:37:42,810 --> 00:37:45,370
But the nice thing about this method, which

677
00:37:45,370 --> 00:37:47,690
is that you don't need to feed the same

678
00:37:47,690 --> 00:37:50,755
prompts to all the models, and this is the beauty of ELO.

679
00:37:50,755 --> 00:37:52,130
In chess, you don't have to play.

680
00:37:52,130 --> 00:37:54,430
Everyone plays everyone else.

681
00:37:54,430 --> 00:37:57,050
It can just be some sparse sample of that.

682
00:37:57,050 --> 00:37:59,230
And you can still derive rankings as long

683
00:37:59,230 --> 00:38:02,270
as the graph is connected.

684
00:38:02,270 --> 00:38:04,550
And this is important because a human is rating

685
00:38:04,550 --> 00:38:06,950
and you can't expect a human to rate all the models

686
00:38:06,950 --> 00:38:10,310
or even more than two models.

687
00:38:10,310 --> 00:38:12,430
And the nice thing as well is that

688
00:38:12,430 --> 00:38:14,450
as new prompts and new models come in,

689
00:38:14,450 --> 00:38:21,030
there's a natural story for making this updating over time.

690
00:38:21,030 --> 00:38:26,790
So let me talk about a different approach.

691
00:38:26,790 --> 00:38:30,710
So this is AlpacaEval from 2023.

692
00:38:30,710 --> 00:38:37,270
There were some instructions derived from various sources.

693
00:38:37,270 --> 00:38:44,130
And the metric here is the win rate against a baseline model.

694
00:38:44,130 --> 00:38:47,030
So you have a model, AlpacaEval says,

695
00:38:47,030 --> 00:38:49,160
I'm going to generate it for a prompt,

696
00:38:49,160 --> 00:38:52,240
generate using let's say baseline model GPT-4

697
00:38:52,240 --> 00:38:57,640
preview at that time and then judge using GPT preview, which

698
00:38:57,640 --> 00:38:59,000
one is better?

699
00:38:59,000 --> 00:39:03,960
So immediately, this raises a question of potential bias.

700
00:39:03,960 --> 00:39:06,920
But let's put that aside for now.

701
00:39:06,920 --> 00:39:09,160
So this is basically an instantiation

702
00:39:09,160 --> 00:39:12,920
of LLM as a judge, which is very popular these days

703
00:39:12,920 --> 00:39:16,200
for evaluating language models.

704
00:39:16,200 --> 00:39:18,120
And you can mitigate this bias by having

705
00:39:18,120 --> 00:39:21,720
multiple judges and ensembling and so on and so forth.

706
00:39:21,720 --> 00:39:24,040
So one problem with initial AlpacaEval

707
00:39:24,040 --> 00:39:29,040
is that LLM judges favor long responses.

708
00:39:29,040 --> 00:39:31,400
And this led to a bunch of leaderboard gaming

709
00:39:31,400 --> 00:39:34,800
where a bunch of models that were fine-tuned were submitted

710
00:39:34,800 --> 00:39:38,520
and they got really high LLM AlpacaEval

711
00:39:38,520 --> 00:39:41,300
performance just because the responses were longer.

712
00:39:41,300 --> 00:39:45,560
So this got fixed in a subsequent paper where

713
00:39:45,560 --> 00:39:51,490
they use a very simple regression method to de-bias the metric.

714
00:39:51,490 --> 00:39:57,370
OK, but this raises a more general question here,

715
00:39:57,370 --> 00:40:00,610
which is not necessarily specific to AlpacaEval which is,

716
00:40:00,610 --> 00:40:02,890
how do you evaluate a metric?

717
00:40:02,890 --> 00:40:06,630
So we've been talking about evaluation of models.

718
00:40:06,630 --> 00:40:09,390
And if I have a metric, how you can evaluate a model.

719
00:40:09,390 --> 00:40:11,450
But if I'm coming up with a metric,

720
00:40:11,450 --> 00:40:14,370
how is this metric any good?

721
00:40:14,370 --> 00:40:17,030
So this is a hard problem.

722
00:40:17,030 --> 00:40:20,890
And there's no answer here.

723
00:40:20,890 --> 00:40:24,730
One thing you can do is more of a sanity check

724
00:40:24,730 --> 00:40:27,210
is you can look at correlation with other metrics

725
00:40:27,210 --> 00:40:33,810
with the idea that higher isn't necessarily better in all cases

726
00:40:33,810 --> 00:40:36,190
unless you are trying to mimic this other metric.

727
00:40:36,190 --> 00:40:41,610
So AlpacaEval correlation with Chatbot Arena

728
00:40:41,610 --> 00:40:46,260
is 0.98 which is quite high.

729
00:40:46,260 --> 00:40:50,820
So which suggests that you could--

730
00:40:50,820 --> 00:40:56,800
let's say you wanted to evaluate on a Chatbot Arena,

731
00:40:56,800 --> 00:40:59,620
but you're too shy to put your model

732
00:40:59,620 --> 00:41:01,880
or you don't want to wait for humans or whatever,

733
00:41:01,880 --> 00:41:07,980
you can use AlpacaEval to evaluate instead.

734
00:41:07,980 --> 00:41:09,520
Now, this correlation, of course,

735
00:41:09,520 --> 00:41:13,700
is with respect to a certain set of models.

736
00:41:13,700 --> 00:41:17,220
And so this might not hold for models

737
00:41:17,220 --> 00:41:22,860
that let's say are stronger than GPT-4 preview.

738
00:41:22,860 --> 00:41:27,940
So at this point, this leaderboard I don't think is really--

739
00:41:27,940 --> 00:41:34,940
hasn't been maintained in over a year but this what it looked like at that time.

740
00:41:34,940 --> 00:41:39,240
So there's another benchmark I'll talk about,

741
00:41:39,240 --> 00:41:45,640
which is WildBench and this one was sourced a bunch of examples

742
00:41:45,640 --> 00:41:48,700
from human-chatbot conversations.

743
00:41:48,700 --> 00:41:53,400
So like Chatbot Arena, they also put up a free service

744
00:41:53,400 --> 00:41:54,960
where people could chat with this bot

745
00:41:54,960 --> 00:42:00,040
and they collected the queries.

746
00:42:00,040 --> 00:42:09,400
And then like AlpacaEval, they used LLM as a judge

747
00:42:09,400 --> 00:42:14,480
to evaluate the model.

748
00:42:14,480 --> 00:42:17,760
The main innovation here is the use

749
00:42:17,760 --> 00:42:21,600
of a checklist, which is generated

750
00:42:21,600 --> 00:42:26,680
and specific to a particular prompt or task.

751
00:42:26,680 --> 00:42:28,880
And the idea here is that if you just

752
00:42:28,880 --> 00:42:34,760
ask a language model to judge whether this response is good

753
00:42:34,760 --> 00:42:35,880
or not.

754
00:42:35,880 --> 00:42:39,400
This is in some sense a very ill-defined task.

755
00:42:39,400 --> 00:42:41,970
I don't know what depends on what you care about.

756
00:42:41,970 --> 00:42:44,850
And so the idea here is that by having a checklist or some

757
00:42:44,850 --> 00:42:50,250
of rubric, this greatly scopes and makes the task evaluation

758
00:42:50,250 --> 00:42:53,530
task more well-defined.

759
00:42:53,530 --> 00:42:57,250
So they did this, also, they showed correlation

760
00:42:57,250 --> 00:43:00,410
with Chatbot Arena which admittedly

761
00:43:00,410 --> 00:43:04,290
is a little bit circular because then you can ask why, well,

762
00:43:04,290 --> 00:43:06,850
is Chatbot Arena really the ground truth?

763
00:43:06,850 --> 00:43:11,410
But at least we're all in the same boat together.

764
00:43:11,410 --> 00:43:16,050
OK, so to conclude this section, how do you

765
00:43:16,050 --> 00:43:20,570
evaluate open-ended responses?

766
00:43:20,570 --> 00:43:24,730
There's no clear solution here but there's some ideas.

767
00:43:24,730 --> 00:43:31,930
Pairwise comparisons between a reference and a model response

768
00:43:31,930 --> 00:43:35,370
is generally a good idea because especially

769
00:43:35,370 --> 00:43:37,370
for very similar responses, you can

770
00:43:37,370 --> 00:43:38,888
give directional information.

771
00:43:38,888 --> 00:43:40,680
Well, this one's slightly better than this.

772
00:43:40,680 --> 00:43:43,900
But in terms of absolute ranking like is this a 7 out of 10

773
00:43:43,900 --> 00:43:45,160
or an 8 out of 10?

774
00:43:45,160 --> 00:43:48,980
That tends to be a lot less high signal.

775
00:43:48,980 --> 00:43:51,300
One should always be aware of biases

776
00:43:51,300 --> 00:43:55,580
both in terms of humans or LLM judges.

777
00:43:55,580 --> 00:43:58,860
They have different biases, but nonetheless that's

778
00:43:58,860 --> 00:44:03,180
something important to pay attention to.

779
00:44:03,180 --> 00:44:05,780
Perhaps the only thing you might be able to do

780
00:44:05,780 --> 00:44:10,380
is you evaluate over multiple judges, humans,

781
00:44:10,380 --> 00:44:13,180
and judges of all everything is saying

782
00:44:13,180 --> 00:44:17,220
your model is better than--maybe your model is better.

783
00:44:17,220 --> 00:44:21,300
And finally, the evaluation problem here

784
00:44:21,300 --> 00:44:23,300
of open-ended response.

785
00:44:23,300 --> 00:44:26,700
I would emphasize is not really well-defined.

786
00:44:26,700 --> 00:44:29,460
And so increasingly, I think it's

787
00:44:29,460 --> 00:44:34,980
important to define rubrics or checklists to improve

788
00:44:34,980 --> 00:44:42,460
the reliability or well-definedness of an actual evaluation.

789
00:44:42,460 --> 00:44:44,980
And this is true regardless of a human and a judge.

790
00:44:44,980 --> 00:44:46,680
So anyone who's done crowdsourcing

791
00:44:46,680 --> 00:44:49,440
knows that if you just ask a human to rate

792
00:44:49,440 --> 00:44:51,700
something without giving some of rubric,

793
00:44:51,700 --> 00:44:55,340
you're probably going to get very nonsensical results.

794
00:44:58,400 --> 00:45:04,800
OK, so let's move on to the agentic world.

795
00:45:04,800 --> 00:45:06,480
So you can think about what we've

796
00:45:06,480 --> 00:45:11,520
been doing so far is evaluate what language models say,

797
00:45:11,520 --> 00:45:12,380
and that's chat.

798
00:45:12,380 --> 00:45:17,200
And now, we're going to evaluate what LLMs do, which is agents.

799
00:45:17,200 --> 00:45:24,240
And so for anyone who's following AI hype,

800
00:45:24,240 --> 00:45:26,340
agents are everywhere.

801
00:45:26,340 --> 00:45:30,440
And these are definitely, I think,

802
00:45:30,440 --> 00:45:34,960
going to transform the way that we think about language models.

803
00:45:34,960 --> 00:45:37,290
So an agent is basically a language model

804
00:45:37,290 --> 00:45:41,050
plus some scaffold, which is a logic for deciding

805
00:45:41,050 --> 00:45:45,650
how the language model is going to be called

806
00:45:45,650 --> 00:45:51,970
and what tools it can have access to and so on and so forth.

807
00:45:51,970 --> 00:45:56,130
So there are a few benchmarks I want to just point out

808
00:45:56,130 --> 00:45:57,890
that are authentic.

809
00:45:57,890 --> 00:46:01,870
So one is very popular.

810
00:46:01,870 --> 00:46:03,350
It's just called SWE-bench.

811
00:46:06,050 --> 00:46:07,970
Basically, the task is you're given

812
00:46:07,970 --> 00:46:12,090
a codebase and a description of a GitHub issue, submit a PR.

813
00:46:12,090 --> 00:46:18,530
And the way you evaluate a PR is whether it passes the unit test.

814
00:46:18,530 --> 00:46:22,770
So these tests are designed so that before you submit a PR,

815
00:46:22,770 --> 00:46:24,710
certain unit tests don't pass.

816
00:46:24,710 --> 00:46:26,230
And then your goal is to fix it.

817
00:46:26,230 --> 00:46:29,410
So certain unit tests pass and you don't break anything else.

818
00:46:29,410 --> 00:46:34,000
So here's an example of some instructions.

819
00:46:34,000 --> 00:46:36,260
Here's the issue.

820
00:46:36,260 --> 00:46:39,880
And then some code that is provided in the context.

821
00:46:39,880 --> 00:46:43,220
And then your goal is to generate a patch.

822
00:46:43,220 --> 00:46:46,820
And given a patch you are evaluated on

823
00:46:46,820 --> 00:46:48,980
whether you pass or not.

824
00:46:48,980 --> 00:46:51,580
So the good thing about this is that the evaluation

825
00:46:51,580 --> 00:46:54,100
is very straightforward.

826
00:46:54,100 --> 00:47:00,620
And let's see how we're doing on SWE-bench.

827
00:47:00,620 --> 00:47:03,420
Actually, this is going to be SWE-bench Verified, which

828
00:47:03,420 --> 00:47:08,500
later I'll show you, tell you why this happened.

829
00:47:08,500 --> 00:47:12,820
So SWE-bench in-- this was only, I

830
00:47:12,820 --> 00:47:22,860
guess 2024, was still around 16% and now it's up to 93.

831
00:47:22,860 --> 00:47:24,040
Good job, Mythos.

832
00:47:31,280 --> 00:47:36,620
So this is a very canonical generic benchmark,

833
00:47:36,620 --> 00:47:39,520
as a lot of the attention on agents

834
00:47:39,520 --> 00:47:42,420
has been invested in coding abilities.

835
00:47:42,420 --> 00:47:45,920
So SWE-bench really pioneered this way

836
00:47:45,920 --> 00:47:48,760
of thinking about how to assess an agent's ability

837
00:47:48,760 --> 00:47:53,800
to write and code in a realistic environment.

838
00:47:53,800 --> 00:47:58,240
So here's another project called Terminal-Bench.

839
00:47:58,240 --> 00:48:01,880
And here the idea is that this is going

840
00:48:01,880 --> 00:48:03,540
to be more general purpose.

841
00:48:03,540 --> 00:48:06,880
And we're going to basically have the environment be

842
00:48:06,880 --> 00:48:08,600
a computer terminal.

843
00:48:08,600 --> 00:48:11,640
And we're going to define a bunch of tasks

844
00:48:11,640 --> 00:48:13,640
that can be done in a computer terminal

845
00:48:13,640 --> 00:48:16,612
by typing in a bunch of commands.

846
00:48:16,612 --> 00:48:18,320
And the nice thing about this environment

847
00:48:18,320 --> 00:48:23,000
is it's very simple and universal.

848
00:48:23,000 --> 00:48:30,970
And these tasks were crowdsourced from 93 people

849
00:48:30,970 --> 00:48:35,210
from across the world.

850
00:48:35,210 --> 00:48:40,930
89 of these tasks constitute Terminal-Bench 2.0.

851
00:48:40,930 --> 00:48:47,170
These tasks generally take ranging from one hour

852
00:48:47,170 --> 00:48:49,330
to over a week, depending on whether you're

853
00:48:49,330 --> 00:48:52,250
an expert or a junior.

854
00:48:52,250 --> 00:48:57,810
And then, here's what the leaderboard looks like as of today.

855
00:48:57,810 --> 00:49:01,370
So top models are the frontier models.

856
00:49:01,370 --> 00:49:07,650
One thing that's interesting is that the agent is also important

857
00:49:07,650 --> 00:49:13,370
here because you can have two agents but the same model

858
00:49:13,370 --> 00:49:17,690
and they have different accuracies.

859
00:49:17,690 --> 00:49:23,150
So another benchmark I'll talk about is Cybersecurity.

860
00:49:23,150 --> 00:49:27,370
So this is 40 Capture The Flag tasks.

861
00:49:27,370 --> 00:49:30,420
And the setting here is that you have

862
00:49:30,420 --> 00:49:35,860
an agent that is given an environment

863
00:49:35,860 --> 00:49:41,220
and it can run various commands like look at the source code.

864
00:49:41,220 --> 00:49:47,960
It can access the web server.

865
00:49:47,960 --> 00:49:50,380
And the goal is to essentially hack into this server

866
00:49:50,380 --> 00:49:53,220
to extract some flag, which is a unique string that

867
00:49:53,220 --> 00:49:56,020
proves that you've hacked into the server.

868
00:49:56,020 --> 00:49:59,940
So these are competitions, cybersecurity exercises that

869
00:49:59,940 --> 00:50:02,060
are done by humans normally.

870
00:50:02,060 --> 00:50:11,580
And this is an example of what the agent

871
00:50:11,580 --> 00:50:13,880
scaffold looks like here.

872
00:50:13,880 --> 00:50:17,060
This is a very simplistic version where basically you

873
00:50:17,060 --> 00:50:19,580
have one continuous memory.

874
00:50:19,580 --> 00:50:25,180
You produce some action, you get environment feedback,

875
00:50:25,180 --> 00:50:27,970
and then you put the results into this buffer

876
00:50:27,970 --> 00:50:29,550
and this is concatenated.

877
00:50:29,550 --> 00:50:33,590
As I'll show you later, this history

878
00:50:33,590 --> 00:50:35,090
obviously grows quite a bit.

879
00:50:35,090 --> 00:50:41,710
And you'll need to have better ways of managing context.

880
00:50:41,710 --> 00:50:46,190
So this is what the leaderboard looks like when it came out.

881
00:50:46,190 --> 00:50:50,390
效果最好的模型大约在 10% 左右。

882
00:50:50,390 --> 00:50:57,270
随着时间推移，现在这个问题其实已经完全解决了。

883
00:50:57,270 --> 00:50:59,670
所以这就是 CyBench。

884
00:50:59,670 --> 00:51:06,070
还有另一类问题，属于 MLE 工程。

885
00:51:06,070 --> 00:51:11,910
这个基准测试包含了一系列 Kaggle 竞赛，

886
00:51:11,910 --> 00:51:14,550
涉及处理数据、训练模型，

887
00:51:14,550 --> 00:51:16,110
等等等等。

888
00:51:16,110 --> 00:51:20,770
智能体可以查看数据集、阅读描述，

889
00:51:20,770 --> 00:51:26,610
编写代码、训练模型，最终提交结果，

890
00:51:26,610 --> 00:51:28,690
然后得到一个评分。

891
00:51:28,690 --> 00:51:32,690
这就是目前的排行榜的样子。

892
00:51:32,690 --> 00:51:37,010
同样，你可以看到，其实还是那些老面孔，

893
00:51:37,010 --> 00:51:39,210
在 LLM 这边。

894
00:51:39,210 --> 00:51:41,050
但在不同的智能体脚手架方面，

895
00:51:41,050 --> 00:51:45,530
存在相当大的差异。

896
00:51:45,530 --> 00:51:47,690
有一点需要注意，我要指出，

897
00:51:47,690 --> 00:51:53,330
关于智能体脚手架，它们非常重要。

898
00:51:53,330 --> 00:51:58,570
所以当我说我讲的是语言模型评估时，

899
00:51:58,570 --> 00:52:02,010
这比语言模型评估的范围更广。

900
00:52:02,010 --> 00:52:07,690
原因在于，不像我之前提到的

901
00:52:07,690 --> 00:52:11,370
最早的那个 CyBench 智能体脚手架，现在

902
00:52:11,370 --> 00:52:14,170
你需要复杂得多才能解决

903
00:52:14,170 --> 00:52:16,770
真正复杂的任务。

904
00:52:16,770 --> 00:52:20,890
人们意识到的一件事是，显式规划

905
00:52:20,890 --> 00:52:21,950
是有帮助的。

906
00:52:21,950 --> 00:52:23,940
你不能只是意识流，

907
00:52:23,940 --> 00:52:24,960
思维链这样。

908
00:52:24,960 --> 00:52:27,340
然后你构建起这个上下文，

909
00:52:27,340 --> 00:52:31,220
智能体很容易就迷失了方向。

910
00:52:31,220 --> 00:52:35,580
所以与其维护一个待办清单，把它逐项勾掉。

911
00:52:35,580 --> 00:52:40,700
分层委派，智能体应该调用其他子智能体，

912
00:52:40,700 --> 00:52:42,160
带有一些干净的上下文。

913
00:52:42,160 --> 00:52:46,260
那个智能体做一些事情，只返回结果。

914
00:52:46,260 --> 00:52:50,640
主智能体不需要看到所有血淋淋的细节，

915
00:52:50,640 --> 00:52:55,900
这有助于封装，提供一些封装。

916
00:52:55,900 --> 00:52:59,420
记忆，人们一直在实验

917
00:52:59,420 --> 00:53:01,560
显式地读写文件，

918
00:53:01,560 --> 00:53:03,400
尤其是随着上下文增长。

919
00:53:03,400 --> 00:53:08,580
你不能只是把它存在上下文窗口里。

920
00:53:08,580 --> 00:53:13,660
最后，还有更多上下文工程，采用

921
00:53:13,660 --> 00:53:16,260
of managing the process.

922
00:53:16,260 --> 00:53:18,580
When should you delegate to sub-agents?

923
00:53:18,580 --> 00:53:23,470
When to try this strategy versus this other strategy, what

924
00:53:23,470 --> 00:53:25,090
to write persistent memory.

925
00:53:25,090 --> 00:53:30,710
And these things are generally optimized

926
00:53:30,710 --> 00:53:36,470
for the particular language model.

927
00:53:36,470 --> 00:53:40,670
So agents are exciting because they enhance the capability

928
00:53:40,670 --> 00:53:42,490
surface of language models.

929
00:53:42,490 --> 00:53:44,130
Scaffolds are very important.

930
00:53:44,130 --> 00:53:47,470
So really, when we talk about evaluating agents,

931
00:53:47,470 --> 00:53:49,950
it's evaluating both the language model

932
00:53:49,950 --> 00:53:52,250
and the agent scaffold.

933
00:53:56,150 --> 00:53:58,910
Now, we're going to switch gears a little bit

934
00:53:58,910 --> 00:54:03,790
and talk about this other very different style of benchmarks.

935
00:54:03,790 --> 00:54:08,390
I call them pure reasoning for lack of a better term.

936
00:54:08,390 --> 00:54:11,590
And the motivation is that all the tasks so far require

937
00:54:11,590 --> 00:54:14,630
some linguistic and world knowledge.

938
00:54:14,630 --> 00:54:17,070
And so the question is, can we just

939
00:54:17,070 --> 00:54:21,450
isolate reasoning or pure fluid intelligence

940
00:54:21,450 --> 00:54:24,330
from a knowledge and knowing facts?

941
00:54:24,330 --> 00:54:26,730
And you could argue that this gives you

942
00:54:26,730 --> 00:54:31,410
a form of more pure intelligence.

943
00:54:31,410 --> 00:54:36,330
So there was this project, ARC-AGI,

944
00:54:36,330 --> 00:54:43,170
which started back in 2019, where the goal was exactly this.

945
00:54:43,170 --> 00:54:48,990
So the task was meant to be 100% solvable by humans,

946
00:54:48,990 --> 00:54:50,490
but challenging for AIs.

947
00:54:50,490 --> 00:54:55,050
And every task is a special snowflake or that's the intent.

948
00:54:55,050 --> 00:54:57,170
And so memorizing a bunch of facts

949
00:54:57,170 --> 00:55:00,890
or solving previous problems shouldn't really

950
00:55:00,890 --> 00:55:02,970
help you that much.

951
00:55:02,970 --> 00:55:06,110
So if you guys are remembering your LLM history,

952
00:55:06,110 --> 00:55:09,310
2019 was pre-LLM.

953
00:55:09,310 --> 00:55:11,890
I mean this was GPT-2 era.

954
00:55:11,890 --> 00:55:15,140
So this was prescient in that regard.

955
00:55:15,140 --> 00:55:17,640
So here's an example of the first iteration of that problem.

956
00:55:17,640 --> 00:55:20,020
You're given these pictures and you're

957
00:55:20,020 --> 00:55:22,700
trying to guess what comes next.

958
00:55:22,700 --> 00:55:27,080
And if you take 10 seconds, you can probably solve this problem.

959
00:55:30,220 --> 00:55:33,980
So you probably guess it's to just fill in the yellow

960
00:55:33,980 --> 00:55:36,540
to form the rectangle.

961
00:55:36,540 --> 00:55:41,980
So then last year, there was an updated version

962
00:55:41,980 --> 00:55:45,860
of this task, which was a bit more complicated.

963
00:55:45,860 --> 00:55:47,360
And you can take a look at this.

964
00:55:51,220 --> 00:55:54,640
So here's the trajectory.

965
00:55:54,640 --> 00:55:58,180
So when ARC-AGI-1 came out--

966
00:56:01,020 --> 00:56:03,340
I think this is actually 0.

967
00:56:03,340 --> 00:56:06,240
So basically models didn't do anything.

968
00:56:06,240 --> 00:56:08,100
So there's GPT-3-- pre-trained model didn't

969
00:56:08,100 --> 00:56:10,580
move the needle at all, right.

970
00:56:10,580 --> 00:56:14,100
And this was exactly as the creators had intended.

971
00:56:14,100 --> 00:56:17,990
Pre-trained models just learn what

972
00:56:17,990 --> 00:56:19,910
the facts are on the internet and maybe learn

973
00:56:19,910 --> 00:56:21,090
linguistic patterns.

974
00:56:21,090 --> 00:56:24,570
But none of that is actually helpful directly,

975
00:56:24,570 --> 00:56:28,670
at least for these ARC-AGI tasks.

976
00:56:28,670 --> 00:56:33,070
And then something happened in 2024.

977
00:56:33,070 --> 00:56:36,610
And this is when OpenAI released their o1 and o3 models.

978
00:56:36,610 --> 00:56:40,390
Then things started taking off pretty abruptly.

979
00:56:40,390 --> 00:56:46,430
And now, ARC-AGI-1 is basically solved.

980
00:56:46,430 --> 00:56:52,990
And even around 2025 when ARC-AGI-2 came out,

981
00:56:52,990 --> 00:56:55,970
this has also-- it's not quite solved,

982
00:56:55,970 --> 00:57:01,550
but it looks like it's on its way to being solved.

983
00:57:01,550 --> 00:57:08,670
So it was really the reasoning capabilities that unlocked this.

984
00:57:08,670 --> 00:57:14,120
And now one could wonder whether all the knowledge

985
00:57:14,120 --> 00:57:19,780
of actual pre-training was necessary for this task.

986
00:57:19,780 --> 00:57:21,800
And certainly, without pre-training

987
00:57:21,800 --> 00:57:24,180
we wouldn't have this explosion of reasoning models.

988
00:57:24,180 --> 00:57:27,180
So arguably it was still important,

989
00:57:27,180 --> 00:57:30,200
but not maybe directly visible for much

990
00:57:30,200 --> 00:57:32,800
of the history of ARC-AGI.

991
00:57:32,800 --> 00:57:36,100
So now, just last month, ARC-AGI-3 comes out.

992
00:57:36,100 --> 00:57:37,720
So you see this familiar pattern.

993
00:57:37,720 --> 00:57:39,860
Our creators look at this and it's like, oh,

994
00:57:39,860 --> 00:57:41,960
time for a new benchmark.

995
00:57:41,960 --> 00:57:45,880
So this is now an interactive environment

996
00:57:45,880 --> 00:57:49,140
where you can go online and you can play this game.

997
00:57:49,140 --> 00:57:50,180
So again, no language.

998
00:57:50,180 --> 00:57:53,440
You're just trying to figure out, based on patterns.

999
00:57:53,440 --> 00:57:55,320
It's kind of fun.

1000
00:57:55,320 --> 00:57:57,760
And now the scores are extremely low.

1001
00:57:57,760 --> 00:58:00,960
So next year when I teach this class,

1002
00:58:00,960 --> 00:58:03,380
I'm sure I'll have to update this slide.

1003
00:58:03,380 --> 00:58:05,400
But we'll see.

1004
00:58:05,400 --> 00:58:10,720
So I think this ARC-AGI line is a really interesting

1005
00:58:10,720 --> 00:58:12,500
one to think about.

1006
00:58:12,500 --> 00:58:16,380
The goal is to disentangle reasoning from knowledge.

1007
00:58:16,380 --> 00:58:18,080
This is really hard to do.

1008
00:58:18,080 --> 00:58:20,197
And it's not--

1009
00:58:20,197 --> 00:58:22,280
I think this is probably the best attempt at this.

1010
00:58:22,280 --> 00:58:27,820
It's not clear whether you can really fully decouple things.

1011
00:58:27,820 --> 00:58:32,000
And certainly, if people really cared about this benchmark,

1012
00:58:32,000 --> 00:58:34,120
I think people probably could--

1013
00:58:34,120 --> 00:58:36,660
people can benchmark I think probably anything.

1014
00:58:36,660 --> 00:58:38,760
So you could design questions like,

1015
00:58:38,760 --> 00:58:41,780
are these-- these still come from some prior

1016
00:58:41,780 --> 00:58:48,300
and not sure there's anything as quote unquote pure as reasoning.

1017
00:58:48,300 --> 00:58:50,320
This is also constrained to human reasoning.

1018
00:58:50,320 --> 00:58:53,460
The explicit goal is to be 100% human solvable

1019
00:58:53,460 --> 00:58:55,780
within some reasonable time.

1020
00:58:55,780 --> 00:58:59,680
So this doesn't really extend to superhuman reasoning,

1021
00:58:59,680 --> 00:59:02,220
for example [INAUDIBLE] winning more gold medals

1022
00:59:02,220 --> 00:59:05,820
or solving open math problems, which arguably are still

1023
00:59:05,820 --> 00:59:07,860
very useful and important.

1024
00:59:07,860 --> 00:59:11,590
But clearly this exposes some gaps in the current models.

1025
00:59:11,590 --> 00:59:15,310
So there is work to do if you think about the goal

1026
00:59:15,310 --> 00:59:18,810
as basically being able to solve all the tasks.

1027
00:59:22,990 --> 00:59:26,690
So this ARC-AGI-3 especially looks quite graphic.

1028
00:59:26,690 --> 00:59:32,870
So is there some multimodal content involved or encoding

1029
00:59:32,870 --> 00:59:36,410
or as a textual representation as well?

1030
00:59:36,410 --> 00:59:41,350
Yeah so the question is this looks very graphical.

1031
00:59:41,350 --> 00:59:44,230
And so what is the input to a language model?

1032
00:59:44,230 --> 00:59:46,950
So this is essentially--I don't know what the--

1033
00:59:46,950 --> 00:59:48,930
I think it's 64 by 64.

1034
00:59:48,930 --> 00:59:51,590
So you can provide this image, or you

1035
00:59:51,590 --> 00:59:58,390
can provide it in ASCII art or some textual representation.

1036
00:59:58,390 --> 01:00:01,750
Either way, there is a spatial element here

1037
01:00:01,750 --> 01:00:05,830
that the model has to reason about, which is really not

1038
01:00:05,830 --> 01:00:07,570
English or natural language.

1039
01:00:12,920 --> 01:00:15,800
So let's shift gears and talk

1040
01:00:15,800 --> 01:00:21,040
about another class of benchmarks which is related to safety.

1041
01:00:21,040 --> 01:00:23,640
So people have been talking about safety

1042
01:00:23,640 --> 01:00:25,960
in language models for a while.

1043
01:00:25,960 --> 01:00:29,520
And if you think about other areas such as cars,

1044
01:00:29,520 --> 01:00:32,280
it's pretty clear what safety means here.

1045
01:00:32,280 --> 01:00:37,340
These ratings for safety ratings for cars,

1046
01:00:37,340 --> 01:00:40,440
which involve testing based on smashing cars

1047
01:00:40,440 --> 01:00:43,040
into a wall with an airbag and seeing

1048
01:00:43,040 --> 01:00:49,520
how well it does and protects the dummies in the car.

1049
01:00:49,520 --> 01:00:53,440
And this is with decades of lobbying and figuring out

1050
01:00:53,440 --> 01:00:55,800
what safety means for vehicles.

1051
01:00:55,800 --> 01:00:57,760
So what does it mean for AI?

1052
01:00:57,760 --> 01:01:01,523
So I would say that there is not a great answer here,

1053
01:01:01,523 --> 01:01:03,440
but I'll give you some examples of what people

1054
01:01:03,440 --> 01:01:05,080
have been thinking about.

1055
01:01:05,080 --> 01:01:08,760
So there's this benchmark called Harm-Bench.

1056
01:01:08,760 --> 01:01:14,140
And this is really about prompting a model with things

1057
01:01:14,140 --> 01:01:20,060
that are meant to be harmful and expecting the model

1058
01:01:20,060 --> 01:01:22,940
to essentially refuse.

1059
01:01:22,940 --> 01:01:25,400
So this is one type of safety.

1060
01:01:25,400 --> 01:01:28,780
And I think probably the dominant thing

1061
01:01:28,780 --> 01:01:30,740
when people think about safety is

1062
01:01:30,740 --> 01:01:34,020
that I want to prevent bad actors from doing things

1063
01:01:34,020 --> 01:01:35,860
with language models, but it turns out

1064
01:01:35,860 --> 01:01:38,080
that's not the only thing that matters.

1065
01:01:38,080 --> 01:01:39,860
So there's this other work, AIR-Bench,

1066
01:01:39,860 --> 01:01:44,460
which tries to think about safety holistically

1067
01:01:44,460 --> 01:01:49,940
by looking at all the regulatory frameworks in the EU, China,

1068
01:01:49,940 --> 01:01:55,140
and the US company policies and builds

1069
01:01:55,140 --> 01:02:00,980
a taxonomy of all the different things that could go wrong.

1070
01:02:00,980 --> 01:02:03,220
And then using this, constructing

1071
01:02:03,220 --> 01:02:08,470
a particular set of prompts and evaluating

1072
01:02:08,470 --> 01:02:12,030
and so on and so forth.

1073
01:02:12,030 --> 01:02:15,730
But one problem with safety is, jailbreaking,

1074
01:02:15,730 --> 01:02:18,470
which is its own sub problem, which

1075
01:02:18,470 --> 01:02:20,990
is that language models are generally trained

1076
01:02:20,990 --> 01:02:23,790
to refuse harmful instructions.

1077
01:02:23,790 --> 01:02:28,590
But as we know you can get around this if you're clever.

1078
01:02:28,590 --> 01:02:30,910
And so there's some early work that

1079
01:02:30,910 --> 01:02:37,630
uses automatic ways to optimize for prompts to bypass safety

1080
01:02:37,630 --> 01:02:43,230
using GCG, which is basically this coordinate

1081
01:02:43,230 --> 01:02:45,350
wise optimization algorithm.

1082
01:02:45,350 --> 01:02:49,990
And remarkably, optimizing on open models

1083
01:02:49,990 --> 01:02:51,690
allows us to transfer to models.

1084
01:02:51,690 --> 01:02:55,490
So I hope this doesn't—these attacks don't work anymore.

1085
01:02:55,490 --> 01:02:59,510
But at the time, you can generate some basically

1086
01:02:59,510 --> 01:03:05,180
gibberish and step by step plan to destroy humanity

1087
01:03:05,180 --> 01:03:10,240
and OpenAI's model will comply.

1088
01:03:10,240 --> 01:03:11,860
Obviously, you could argue, well,

1089
01:03:11,860 --> 01:03:16,960
is this actually a harmful plan to destroy humanity?

1090
01:03:16,960 --> 01:03:19,840
At least it did not—the expected behavior is that it

1091
01:03:19,840 --> 01:03:22,240
should probably refuse that.

1092
01:03:22,240 --> 01:03:28,680
So this raises a bigger problem which is, what is safety?

1093
01:03:28,680 --> 01:03:30,720
And one thing that makes this tricky

1094
01:03:30,720 --> 01:03:35,240
is that many aspects of safety are very contextual.

1095
01:03:35,240 --> 01:03:39,940
So it involves politics, law, social norms.

1096
01:03:39,940 --> 01:03:43,120
This varies across different countries.

1097
01:03:43,120 --> 01:03:45,580
And then also, if you think about safety,

1098
01:03:45,580 --> 01:03:48,680
you have to think about what are the risks.

1099
01:03:48,680 --> 01:03:53,480
And the risks are actually quite varied as well

1100
01:03:53,480 --> 01:03:55,820
and demand probably different attention.

1101
01:03:55,820 --> 01:03:58,520
So hallucinations is a risk, especially

1102
01:03:58,520 --> 01:04:03,180
let's say in medical or legal or financial settings.

1103
01:04:03,180 --> 01:04:06,720
But this is also correlated with just capabilities.

1104
01:04:06,720 --> 01:04:09,340
If you make a model more accurate,

1105
01:04:09,340 --> 01:04:12,060
it's correlated with not who is seeing as much.

1106
01:04:12,060 --> 01:04:15,220
Sycophancy is a problem.

1107
01:04:15,220 --> 01:04:16,920
Abetting crimes is a problem.

1108
01:04:16,920 --> 01:04:23,500
But this might be counter to capabilities, inequality.

1109
01:04:23,500 --> 01:04:29,140
Losing critical thinking might be a risk as well that correlates

1110
01:04:29,140 --> 01:04:30,040
with capability.

1111
01:04:30,040 --> 01:04:35,060
So if you think about the societal impact of language

1112
01:04:35,060 --> 01:04:39,180
models on the world and think about safety

1113
01:04:39,180 --> 01:04:42,680
as ensuring that AI goes well for people,

1114
01:04:42,680 --> 01:04:46,580
this is actually a much more complex topic

1115
01:04:46,580 --> 01:04:48,800
than I'll have time to talk about.

1116
01:04:48,800 --> 01:04:53,240
And finally, there is a dual use aspect of language models.

1117
01:04:53,240 --> 01:04:56,860
So the cybersecurity agents can be used to hack into a system

1118
01:04:56,860 --> 01:05:00,630
or to do penetration testing and make systems more secure, which

1119
01:05:00,630 --> 01:05:04,630
makes talking about whether these are safety risks

1120
01:05:04,630 --> 01:05:09,750
or actually beneficial a double-edged sword.

1121
01:05:09,750 --> 01:05:14,990
OK, so let's move on now to talk about some broader

1122
01:05:14,990 --> 01:05:17,610
considerations of evaluation.

1123
01:05:17,610 --> 01:05:23,790
So one thing that I think is an important aspect of evaluation

1124
01:05:23,790 --> 01:05:27,390
is realism or ecological validity.

1125
01:05:27,390 --> 01:05:30,390
How well does evaluation capture real-world use?

1126
01:05:30,390 --> 01:05:37,230
So in particular exams are very far away from real-world use.

1127
01:05:37,230 --> 01:05:40,430
Chatbot Arena are from real people

1128
01:05:40,430 --> 01:05:44,390
but one could wonder whether this distribution is

1129
01:05:44,390 --> 01:05:48,510
the right distribution of people or use cases or not.

1130
01:05:48,510 --> 01:05:51,030
There's a few benchmarks I'll mention

1131
01:05:51,030 --> 01:05:55,170
that try to get at ecological validity,

1132
01:05:55,170 --> 01:05:58,140
and in particular at the use case level,

1133
01:05:58,140 --> 01:06:00,400
at least not the individual query level.

1134
01:06:00,400 --> 01:06:05,640
So OpenAI created benchmark called GDPVal.

1135
01:06:05,640 --> 01:06:08,640
They looked at all the top nine sectors

1136
01:06:08,640 --> 01:06:13,080
according to US GDP, 44 different occupations,

1137
01:06:13,080 --> 01:06:17,080
got a bunch of professionals to create tasks.

1138
01:06:17,080 --> 01:06:20,920
These are professionals with about 14 years of experience.

1139
01:06:20,920 --> 01:06:25,840
And you can see people who are nurses who work concierge,

1140
01:06:25,840 --> 01:06:28,960
real estate agents, film and video editors

1141
01:06:28,960 --> 01:06:30,600
and so on and so forth.

1142
01:06:30,600 --> 01:06:35,360
And they created a bunch of tasks.

1143
01:06:35,360 --> 01:06:37,520
Here's another example.

1144
01:06:37,520 --> 01:06:42,100
In the medical domain where up until this point,

1145
01:06:42,100 --> 01:06:45,640
a lot of medical benchmarks were based on standardized exams.

1146
01:06:45,640 --> 01:06:50,180
And in fact, actually for humans, this is also what happens.

1147
01:06:50,180 --> 01:06:51,800
You take these standardized exams

1148
01:06:51,800 --> 01:06:56,242
and you graduate from med school and then you go operate on.

1149
01:06:56,242 --> 01:06:57,950
Well, actually, there's a longer process.

1150
01:06:57,950 --> 01:06:59,790
You don't operate on patients directly.

1151
01:07:03,810 --> 01:07:06,470
But the point is that for a language model,

1152
01:07:06,470 --> 01:07:09,290
you should also not just pass medical exams

1153
01:07:09,290 --> 01:07:11,830
and deploy them directly.

1154
01:07:11,830 --> 01:07:18,490
So this was a project where 121 tasks were

1155
01:07:18,490 --> 01:07:21,610
sourced from 29 clinicians.

1156
01:07:21,610 --> 01:07:25,410
And this was meant to represent the type of things

1157
01:07:25,410 --> 01:07:28,450
that clinicians would actually ask to a language model, which

1158
01:07:28,450 --> 01:07:31,490
are quite different than the multiple choice

1159
01:07:31,490 --> 01:07:34,410
type of exam questions.

1160
01:07:34,410 --> 01:07:39,870
Here's another project which gets at real data.

1161
01:07:39,870 --> 01:07:43,010
So if you ask, who actually has the data

1162
01:07:43,010 --> 01:07:44,230
on what people are doing?

1163
01:07:44,230 --> 01:07:46,810
Well, it's the model developers.

1164
01:07:46,810 --> 01:07:48,970
And so this project is interesting

1165
01:07:48,970 --> 01:07:55,750
because it uses language models themselves to analyze real data.

1166
01:07:55,750 --> 01:07:58,490
So in particular, you do to privacy.

1167
01:07:58,490 --> 01:08:02,370
You can't literally go and look at people's data,

1168
01:08:02,370 --> 01:08:04,990
but you can have language models look at the data

1169
01:08:04,990 --> 01:08:08,030
and analyze and summarize general patterns.

1170
01:08:08,030 --> 01:08:10,750
So then the language models could tell you

1171
01:08:10,750 --> 01:08:13,990
about what are the types of people--

1172
01:08:13,990 --> 01:08:17,450
what are the types of things that people use Claude for?

1173
01:08:20,470 --> 01:08:24,350
And sometimes realism and privacy

1174
01:08:24,350 --> 01:08:27,109
are at odds with each other because ideally you

1175
01:08:27,109 --> 01:08:31,609
would want actually a sample from the actual query stream.

1176
01:08:31,609 --> 01:08:33,990
And that would be very valuable to assess

1177
01:08:33,990 --> 01:08:36,350
how well a model is working and understand deeply

1178
01:08:36,350 --> 01:08:37,510
what the errors are.

1179
01:08:37,510 --> 01:08:43,830
But then that runs into privacy considerations.

1180
01:08:43,830 --> 01:08:46,670
OK, so another consideration, which

1181
01:08:46,670 --> 01:08:51,899
is another type of validity is just in general,

1182
01:08:51,899 --> 01:08:55,880
how do that the evaluations are scientifically valid?

1183
01:08:55,880 --> 01:08:59,920
And this was brought up fairly early, train-test overlap

1184
01:08:59,920 --> 01:09:01,680
or contamination.

1185
01:09:01,680 --> 01:09:04,319
So in a machine learning 101, you

1186
01:09:04,319 --> 01:09:08,960
learn don't train on your test set hopefully.

1187
01:09:08,960 --> 01:09:11,279
And before foundation models this

1188
01:09:11,279 --> 01:09:15,060
was actually pretty clear right.

1189
01:09:15,060 --> 01:09:19,080
So with ImageNet and SQuAD and many benchmarks

1190
01:09:19,080 --> 01:09:21,560
sorry, data sets, there was always a train split

1191
01:09:21,560 --> 01:09:24,380
and a test split and everyone played the same game.

1192
01:09:24,380 --> 01:09:28,680
You train on your training data, you test on your test data.

1193
01:09:28,680 --> 01:09:30,160
Life was simple.

1194
01:09:30,160 --> 01:09:32,240
And now, you have these models that

1195
01:09:32,240 --> 01:09:34,300
are trained on the internet.

1196
01:09:34,300 --> 01:09:36,880
And much more than that.

1197
01:09:36,880 --> 01:09:39,700
And furthermore, you don't what's in the data.

1198
01:09:39,700 --> 01:09:44,520
So this makes evaluation quite different I think.

1199
01:09:44,520 --> 01:09:46,960
So what can you do in this?

1200
01:09:46,960 --> 01:09:50,810
So first thing you can do is you can

1201
01:09:50,810 --> 01:09:56,970
try to infer whether a model has actually seen the test data.

1202
01:09:56,970 --> 01:10:03,410
So this is a neat idea from Tatsu's group

1203
01:10:03,410 --> 01:10:07,890
where you leverage the fact that the order

1204
01:10:07,890 --> 01:10:11,650
in which the questions in the benchmark occur

1205
01:10:11,650 --> 01:10:14,130
should be random.

1206
01:10:14,130 --> 01:10:19,210
But if the model prefers some particular order that's

1207
01:10:19,210 --> 01:10:22,010
consistent with the benchmark, that is probably--

1208
01:10:22,010 --> 01:10:26,970
it's just trained on that benchmark.

1209
01:10:26,970 --> 01:10:35,770
Another route is that you can encourage people to report

1210
01:10:35,770 --> 01:10:38,370
actual train-test overlap.

1211
01:10:38,370 --> 01:10:41,090
So this has to do with norms.

1212
01:10:41,090 --> 01:10:44,790
I think in general, especially maybe less in machine learning,

1213
01:10:44,790 --> 01:10:47,550
but more in statistics you always

1214
01:10:47,550 --> 01:10:50,070
report confidence intervals.

1215
01:10:50,070 --> 01:10:51,010
You have an estimate.

1216
01:10:51,010 --> 01:10:53,890
And I always want to know, how reliable is that estimate?

1217
01:10:53,890 --> 01:10:55,630
This is just standard practice.

1218
01:10:55,630 --> 01:10:59,190
And so the idea of this position paper

1219
01:10:59,190 --> 01:11:02,010
is that you should model providers.

1220
01:11:02,010 --> 01:11:06,030
If you're going to claim some GPQA score,

1221
01:11:06,030 --> 01:11:09,870
you should always provide some justification

1222
01:11:09,870 --> 01:11:13,490
that you didn't train on the test set.

1223
01:11:16,750 --> 01:11:19,970
So another route is that where you can just give up.

1224
01:11:19,970 --> 01:11:23,863
It's like OK, all these evaluations there--

1225
01:11:23,863 --> 01:11:26,030
let's just assume that people trained on them, let's

1226
01:11:26,030 --> 01:11:28,990
assume the worst case.

1227
01:11:28,990 --> 01:11:31,270
So the thing that we have on our side

1228
01:11:31,270 --> 01:11:33,570
is that you can always define fresh evals.

1229
01:11:33,570 --> 01:11:38,070
So there's a bunch of ideas, LiveCodeBench, UncheeatableEval,

1230
01:11:38,070 --> 01:11:40,910
which basically scrape new web pages or archive

1231
01:11:40,910 --> 01:11:46,520
papers or GitHub and defines evaluations populations that

1232
01:11:46,520 --> 01:11:50,760
are past the cutoff date of the language models.

1233
01:11:50,760 --> 01:11:54,840
So this seems pretty robust and good.

1234
01:11:54,840 --> 01:11:57,360
But one thing to keep in mind is that time stamps

1235
01:11:57,360 --> 01:12:00,880
aren't always safe either, because I might

1236
01:12:00,880 --> 01:12:05,220
post with code for example.

1237
01:12:05,220 --> 01:12:08,280
I might put something on GitHub which is actually

1238
01:12:08,280 --> 01:12:11,320
derived from some other repo.

1239
01:12:11,320 --> 01:12:14,180
And so how do that's actually generally fresh?

1240
01:12:17,360 --> 01:12:21,140
Route 4 is to use private evaluations.

1241
01:12:21,140 --> 01:12:23,860
So companies' model developers do this, right.

1242
01:12:23,860 --> 01:12:26,520
So if you're Google or OpenAI, you have your internal code

1243
01:12:26,520 --> 01:12:29,340
base, which you assume is not on the internet,

1244
01:12:29,340 --> 01:12:34,840
and you obviously wouldn't train on this data,

1245
01:12:34,840 --> 01:12:37,000
so you can use it for evaluation.

1246
01:12:37,000 --> 01:12:40,400
So this generally is pretty reliable.

1247
01:12:40,400 --> 01:12:44,610
And if you're not a company, you can use your personal writings.

1248
01:12:44,610 --> 01:12:47,410
I have a bunch of rejected papers from when

1249
01:12:47,410 --> 01:12:49,950
I was in grad school that I never put online,

1250
01:12:49,950 --> 01:12:52,490
so I can use those.

1251
01:12:52,490 --> 01:12:56,590
And this type of evaluation, I think,

1252
01:12:56,590 --> 01:13:00,130
is very favorable for perplexity evals,

1253
01:13:00,130 --> 01:13:01,770
because all you need in perplexity

1254
01:13:01,770 --> 01:13:04,970
is just you need a good data set and you

1255
01:13:04,970 --> 01:13:09,130
can evaluate log probabilities.

1256
01:13:09,130 --> 01:13:16,970
So another concern that I'll mention is data set quality.

1257
01:13:16,970 --> 01:13:22,610
So remember I showed the leaderboard for SWE-Bench Verified.

1258
01:13:22,610 --> 01:13:26,010
This is because, well, SWE-Bench had

1259
01:13:26,010 --> 01:13:30,970
a bunch of problems or tasks, unit tests that

1260
01:13:30,970 --> 01:13:36,770
weren't quite rigorous enough and other things that got fixed.

1261
01:13:36,770 --> 01:13:44,150
And there are many other benchmarks that have gone

1262
01:13:44,150 --> 01:13:49,070
through this process like GSM8K or MMLU,

1263
01:13:49,070 --> 01:13:54,670
where people have gone and done a more careful audit and realized

1264
01:13:54,670 --> 01:13:59,230
that, wait a minute, this question actually doesn't--

1265
01:13:59,230 --> 01:14:02,510
something's broken around this question.

1266
01:14:02,510 --> 01:14:04,090
So like this one.

1267
01:14:04,090 --> 01:14:07,410
There's no curve given in this text

1268
01:14:07,410 --> 01:14:09,210
so you can't answer this question.

1269
01:14:12,190 --> 01:14:14,310
Does the baby have socks on?

1270
01:14:14,310 --> 01:14:16,230
No, there's no way to tell.

1271
01:14:16,230 --> 01:14:21,623
And there's also this paper that showed that there's

1272
01:14:21,623 --> 01:14:23,790
a lot of problems with the agentic benchmarks, which

1273
01:14:23,790 --> 01:14:26,790
arguably are even harder to assess because it's not just

1274
01:14:26,790 --> 01:14:30,190
you can look at the question and the possible answers

1275
01:14:30,190 --> 01:14:34,110
and you can look at it, but there's a whole environment.

1276
01:14:34,110 --> 01:14:39,190
And there are cases where with coding you can

1277
01:14:39,190 --> 01:14:43,340
it's great to have test cases that either pass or not passed.

1278
01:14:43,340 --> 01:14:46,560
But as we all test cases can be incomplete.

1279
01:14:46,560 --> 01:14:48,120
So you might pass all the test cases

1280
01:14:48,120 --> 01:14:50,960
but still not have a working solution.

1281
01:14:50,960 --> 01:14:53,520
There's another case of this TorchBench benchmark

1282
01:14:53,520 --> 01:14:57,780
where they show that if an agent just outputs the empty response,

1283
01:14:57,780 --> 01:15:00,280
they can get a 38%.

1284
01:15:00,280 --> 01:15:03,600
So there are some trivialities with these benchmarks

1285
01:15:03,600 --> 01:15:07,095
that you want to be very careful to avoid.

1286
01:15:07,095 --> 01:15:08,720
One thing I'll call out is that there's

1287
01:15:08,720 --> 01:15:12,880
this tool called Docent that uses

1288
01:15:12,880 --> 01:15:17,480
language models to inspect agent traces to detect problems.

1289
01:15:17,480 --> 01:15:19,600
So this is a qualitative response

1290
01:15:19,600 --> 01:15:23,800
to our very quantitative heavy way of benchmarking.

1291
01:15:23,800 --> 01:15:25,960
And I would always recommend that whenever

1292
01:15:25,960 --> 01:15:29,200
you do a benchmark, either develop a benchmark

1293
01:15:29,200 --> 01:15:33,680
or run a model on the benchmark that you always

1294
01:15:33,680 --> 01:15:36,855
look at the output and try to audit it to make sure

1295
01:15:36,855 --> 01:15:38,730
that you actually think you're measuring what

1296
01:15:38,730 --> 01:15:41,650
you think you're measuring.

1297
01:15:41,650 --> 01:15:45,670
OK, so maybe getting a bit more philosophical.

1298
01:15:45,670 --> 01:15:47,610
So what's the point of evaluation?

1299
01:15:47,610 --> 01:15:48,110
OK.

1300
01:15:48,110 --> 01:15:51,650
So I've talked a lot about different types of evaluation.

1301
01:15:51,650 --> 01:15:55,670
And really, there's no one evaluation to rule them all.

1302
01:15:55,670 --> 01:15:58,290
And I think it's-- when you're thinking about evaluation,

1303
01:15:58,290 --> 01:16:01,830
you have to be very clear about what the purpose is.

1304
01:16:01,830 --> 01:16:05,410
And I think often this is not really stated clearly.

1305
01:16:05,410 --> 01:16:08,590
So for example, you could be a user,

1306
01:16:08,590 --> 01:16:11,110
or a company that's trying to make a purchase decision.

1307
01:16:11,110 --> 01:16:14,850
Are you going to go with A or B for a particular use case?

1308
01:16:14,850 --> 01:16:17,010
Or you're just a researcher and you

1309
01:16:17,010 --> 01:16:19,730
have this intuitive notion of intelligence

1310
01:16:19,730 --> 01:16:22,330
and you want to measure it somehow.

1311
01:16:22,330 --> 01:16:25,210
Or you want to understand the benefits and harms for business

1312
01:16:25,210 --> 01:16:26,650
or policy reasons.

1313
01:16:26,650 --> 01:16:28,890
Or you're a developer and you want to get feedback

1314
01:16:28,890 --> 01:16:30,930
to improve the model.

1315
01:16:30,930 --> 01:16:33,050
So each of these goals will lead you

1316
01:16:33,050 --> 01:16:38,300
to potentially different benchmarks or some combination

1317
01:16:38,300 --> 01:16:41,060
of evaluation strategies.

1318
01:16:41,060 --> 01:16:43,560
And finally, one thing to address,

1319
01:16:43,560 --> 01:16:45,380
which I hinted at briefly, is that

1320
01:16:45,380 --> 01:16:47,080
what are we even evaluating?

1321
01:16:47,080 --> 01:16:51,820
So before foundation models, researchers evaluated methods

1322
01:16:51,820 --> 01:16:53,960
because we have these train test splits.

1323
01:16:53,960 --> 01:16:57,780
And the only thing that was varying is the actual algorithm.

1324
01:16:57,780 --> 01:17:01,020
And today, we're mostly evaluating models and systems

1325
01:17:01,020 --> 01:17:03,300
where anything goes.

1326
01:17:03,300 --> 01:17:04,800
There are some exceptions.

1327
01:17:04,800 --> 01:17:08,180
So the NanoGPT speedrun is deliberately

1328
01:17:08,180 --> 01:17:12,300
a way to evaluate an algorithm, in particular, how fast you

1329
01:17:12,300 --> 01:17:14,000
can train a model.

1330
01:17:14,000 --> 01:17:17,820
But most of language model evaluation

1331
01:17:17,820 --> 01:17:22,180
is about evaluating the actual end model, which is useful

1332
01:17:22,180 --> 01:17:25,100
because that is the thing that is actually going to get shipped

1333
01:17:25,100 --> 01:17:26,680
and people are going to use.

1334
01:17:26,680 --> 01:17:29,740
But I think the punchline is that you should always

1335
01:17:29,740 --> 01:17:37,480
be very deliberate about declaring what your goals are.

1336
01:17:37,480 --> 01:17:40,700
OK, so there's no one true evaluation.

1337
01:17:40,700 --> 01:17:44,480
Choose what you're trying to measure.

1338
01:17:44,480 --> 01:17:48,680
And we talked about a bunch of different benchmarks

1339
01:17:48,680 --> 01:17:53,560
starting with perplexity, exam based, chat, agentic, reasoning,

1340
01:17:53,560 --> 01:17:55,440
and safety.

1341
01:17:55,440 --> 01:18:00,080
They vary in terms of difficulty, realism,

1342
01:18:00,080 --> 01:18:01,280
how valid they are.

1343
01:18:01,280 --> 01:18:02,580
And there's often trade offs.

1344
01:18:02,580 --> 01:18:07,600
It's hard to have really real and difficult and ecologically

1345
01:18:07,600 --> 01:18:08,140
valid.

1346
01:18:08,140 --> 01:18:10,060
And no trade tests contamination.

1347
01:18:10,060 --> 01:18:13,300
So often you have to compromise on one of these factors,

1348
01:18:13,300 --> 01:18:15,080
and depending on your goals, you will

1349
01:18:15,080 --> 01:18:19,200
have to choose which one you're willing to compromise on.

1350
01:18:19,200 --> 01:18:22,300
So that is the end of this lecture.

1351
01:18:22,300 --> 01:18:23,760
So next week, we're going to start

1352
01:18:23,760 --> 01:18:26,520
talking about training data.

1353
01:18:26,520 --> 01:18:27,660
OK, see you next time.
